# orchestration.config — single source of truth for every project-specific
# literal the orchestration hooks need. This is the ONE file an adopter edits
# to point the generic mechanical layer at their own repository. The hooks
# under scripts/hooks/ source this file at run time; nothing else in the
# mechanical layer carries a project literal.
#
# FORMAT: plain bash. Sourced by every hook that needs a value. Keep it to
# simple `NAME="value"` assignments (space-separated lists where noted). No
# logic, no secrets — this file is COMMITTED, so never put credentials here.
#
# SENSIBLE FAILURE: if this file is missing, or a required value is left blank,
# the affected hook degrades safely (the write-guard becomes a visible no-op
# with a stderr notice rather than silently blocking or silently allowing) so a
# half-configured engine fails loud, not dangerous. Fill the placeholders below
# before relying on enforcement.
#
# NOT HERE — but just as critical: `.claude/settings.local.json` (committed)
# carries two non-hook keys, `env.CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS: "1"`
# and `worktree.baseRef: "head"`. NEVER remove either — their absence caused a
# real incident (deleted env key -> orchestrator saw/spawned ZERO teammates at
# the next session start, no error shown). See README.md's "Critical
# configuration — never remove" section; enforced by
# scripts/hooks/contract-integrity-probe.sh (Layers I/J) and
# scripts/hooks/install.sh (refuses to generate settings.json without them).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROTECTED_PATHS  (used by: guard-main-checkout-writes.sh, contract-integrity-probe.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated, repo-root-relative directory prefixes whose SOURCE may be
# edited ONLY inside an isolated git worktree — never in the shared main
# checkout. These are your product source trees. The write-guard blocks any
# Write/Edit landing at "<main-checkout>/<prefix>/..." so a teammate that
# drifts into the shared checkout is stopped at the moment of the write.
#
# REPLACE the sample below with your repo's real source trees, e.g.
#   PROTECTED_PATHS="frontend backend"
#   PROTECTED_PATHS="src"
#   PROTECTED_PATHS="apps packages"
PROTECTED_PATHS="app packages"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_ROOT / NATIVE_ANDROID_ROOT / NATIVE_IOS_ROOT
# (used by: skill prose only — skills/android-native-dev, skills/ios-native-dev,
# skills/appium-vision-mobile-testing, skills/mobile-release-and-build-ops,
# skills/native-client-visual-qa)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These three are NOT read by any hook at runtime — unlike every other value in
# this file, nothing sources them programmatically. They exist so an adopter
# has exactly one place to fill in the path literals the engine's shipped
# native/mobile skills cite in prose (e.g. "Use this skill for Android work in
# `${APP_ROOT}`"). Leave any of them blank if the corresponding skill doesn't
# apply to your project (e.g. you ship no iOS app) — an unfilled reference in
# a skill you never use is harmless.
#
# REPLACE the samples below with your repo's real native source roots, e.g.
#   APP_ROOT="mobile"
#   NATIVE_ANDROID_ROOT="apps/android"
#   NATIVE_IOS_ROOT="apps/ios"
APP_ROOT=""
NATIVE_ANDROID_ROOT=""
NATIVE_IOS_ROOT=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_TEAMS_DIR  (used by: guard-worktree-isolation.sh, guard-resume-isolation.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Directory under which the harness writes each session's team state —
# session-<first8>/config.json (the live roster), plus the guard-maintained
# spawned-names.log and the idle-events.jsonl / task-events.jsonl event logs.
# The spawn guard reads it to enforce no-name-reuse (name history = roster ∪
# ledger ∪ event logs) and the resume guard reads it to prove a SendMessage
# recipient is a live teammate before allowing a resume.
#
# Leave BLANK to use the platform default ($HOME/.claude/teams) — correct for a
# standard Claude Code install. Set an absolute path only if your harness stores
# session teams somewhere non-standard.
#
# HARNESS-VERSION NOTE: both guards assume the current harness team schema —
# a session dir named "session-<first-8-of-session-id>" holding a config.json
# with a "members" array of { name, agentId, cwd, status } objects, and native
# worktree members whose cwd lives under .claude/worktrees/. If a future harness
# changes that shape, update the resolvers in the two guards (both fail SAFE:
# the spawn guard fails OPEN on a resolver error so an IO glitch never invents a
# name collision; the resume guard fails CLOSED so an unverifiable recipient is
# blocked, with resume-ack: as the override).
SESSION_TEAMS_DIR=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READONLY_ALLOWLIST  (used by: guard-worktree-isolation.sh, detect-nonnative-worktree.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated subagent_types that are READ-ONLY and therefore exempt from
# the worktree-isolation spawn contract (they never write repo files). The
# defaults are Claude Code's built-in read-only agent types — platform-generic,
# safe to keep as-is. Add a custom read-only role here only once you are certain
# it never writes files.
#
# THIS LIST ANSWERS ONE QUESTION ONLY: does this agent need an isolated
# worktree? It is NOT a permission to STAFF delegated work to these types —
# that question is CLAUSE 5 below (HARNESS_UTILITY_TYPES / GENERIC_AGENT_TYPES),
# and it is enforced in code, because it used to be enforced by this comment
# and a comment does not hold.
READONLY_ALLOWLIST="Explore Plan claude-code-guide statusline-setup"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEAL_READONLY_TOOLS / SEAL_WAIT_SECONDS  — THE LOCK-OUT
# (used by: guard-sealed-worktree.sh; docs/plans/worktree-spec-2026-09-11.md,
#  points 3 and 9)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A worker with NO REGISTRATION — its spawn's registration was not written by
# PreToolUse[Agent], SubagentStart or PostToolUse[Agent] — may use ONLY the
# tools named here, so it can read and report why it stopped; everything else
# — Bash, Agent, every file editor, notebooks, unknown tools, MCP tools — is
# refused. A FINISHED worker is refused every tool, these included. This is an
# ALLOWLIST of proven read-only tools, and it is deny-by-default: a tool not
# named here is treated as potentially writing. Do not add Bash.
SEAL_READONLY_TOOLS="Read Glob Grep LS WebFetch WebSearch ListAgents TaskList TaskGet TodoRead ToolSearch"
# How long a worker's first call may wait for a registration that is still
# being written by its own spawn's hooks (they arrive in either order).
# Bounded so the lock-out can never deadlock a worker.
SEAL_WAIT_SECONDS=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARNESS_UTILITY_TYPES / GENERIC_AGENT_TYPES  — the CLAUSE 5 staffing gate
# (used by: guard-worktree-isolation.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On 2026-09-02 an engine-wide audit was dispatched to `Explore`, a generic
# built-in, because a roster teammate would have needed a worktree created
# first and the built-in could be dispatched immediately. Momentum over
# correctness. Nothing refused it: READONLY_ALLOWLIST had exempted the type
# from ISOLATION, and the exemption was read as permission to staff work there.
# Work sent to a generic agent is invisible in the team display and leaves no
# committed artifact, so it disappears from the record.
#
# Clause 5 therefore gates STAFFING separately from isolation. It fires for any
# subagent_type on READONLY_ALLOWLIST or on GENERIC_AGENT_TYPES, minus anything
# on HARNESS_UTILITY_TYPES, and refuses the spawn unless the prompt carries a
# live line
#     generic-agent: <why no roster teammate fits this work>
# with a real reason (>= 30 characters, >= 5 words, >= 3 substantive words, and
# not a speed/convenience argument). Accepted uses are logged to
# .claude/state/generic-agent-dispatches.log.
#
# HARNESS_UTILITY_TYPES — types that configure or explain the HARNESS itself and
# never carry delegated project work. There is no roster teammate who should
# have had a statusline change, so demanding a justification for one is how a
# defense becomes a nuisance and then a formality typed by reflex. Keep this
# list to genuine utilities; the default is deny, so a type newly added to
# READONLY_ALLOWLIST needs the hatch until it is declared a utility here.
HARNESS_UTILITY_TYPES="claude-code-guide statusline-setup output-style-setup"

# GENERIC_AGENT_TYPES — generic built-ins that are NOT on the read-only
# allowlist and so pass the whole spawn contract today. `general-purpose` is the
# obvious detour around clause 5 and is closed by default. Roster teammate types
# never belong here.
GENERIC_AGENT_TYPES="general-purpose"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALLOWED_MODELS  (used by: guard-worktree-isolation.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Space-separated set of model aliases the harness accepts on an Agent spawn's
# `model` parameter, and therefore the only valid <model> tokens in a teammate
# name (see the naming contract: <role>-<model>-<identifier>). The spawn guard
# (clause 2) requires the middle name segment to be one of these AND to
# truthfully name the model the instance boots on — an explicit per-spawn
# `model:` override wins, else the model: frontmatter of the LIVE agent
# definition (.claude/agents/<type>.md). Defaults to Claude Code's aliases
# (opus/sonnet/haiku), plus fable — a real, harness-accepted model alias some
# adopters run design/creative rounds on; edit to match your harness (e.g. add
# another tier alias). `inherit` is a valid override VALUE but is NOT a
# concrete model, so it is never a valid NAME token — a model:"inherit" spawn
# is treated as undeterminable and accepts any valid alias.
ALLOWED_MODELS="fable opus sonnet haiku"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL_TIERS  (used by: guard-worktree-isolation.sh clause 6,
#               contract-integrity-probe.sh Layer MT; parsed ONLY through
#               scripts/lib/model-tiers.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MODEL CAPABILITY ORDER, AS DATA. Tiers separated by `>`, most capable
# tier LEFTMOST; aliases inside one tier are separated by spaces and are EQUAL
# (moving between them, either way, is neither an upgrade nor a downgrade).
# The alias set MUST equal ALLOWED_MODELS above — the integrity probe refuses
# a mismatch, and the spawn guard fails OPEN (with a notice) on an alias it
# cannot rank, because "unknown" is not "forbidden".
#
# WHAT THIS DECLARATION FEEDS: the spawn guard's clause 6 refuses an explicit
# per-spawn `model:` that lands on a LOWER tier than the teammate definition's
# own `model:` default, unless the prompt carries a live
#     model-downgrade-ack: <reason>
# line (logged to .claude/state/model-downgrade-acks.log). Equal or higher
# tier is always silent. The doctrine file quotes this value verbatim and the
# probe checks the quotation against this line, so prose cannot drift from it.
#
# RE-DERIVE THIS LINE WHEN A MODEL IS ADDED, RETIRED OR RE-RANKED. Do it here,
# once, from what is actually known about the model — never by editing a
# consumer, and NEVER by letting a consumer infer capability from an alias's
# name, novelty or position in ALLOWED_MODELS. On 2026-09-02 an orchestrator
# inferred exactly that (sonnet -> fable read as a downgrade; it is the
# reverse), killed a correctly-configured teammate on the inference, and
# commissioned a guard to make the inference permanent. This line is the fix:
# a fact nobody is permitted to guess at.
MODEL_TIERS="fable > opus > sonnet > haiku"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MODEL_CEILING  (used by: guard-model-ceiling.sh,
#                 contract-integrity-probe.sh Layer MC; parsed ONLY through
#                 scripts/lib/model-tiers.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE COST CEILING, AS DATA — AND IT IS A DIFFERENT ORDER FROM THE ONE ABOVE.
# MODEL_TIERS answers which model is STRONGER. It has never answered which
# model to SPEND, and the two orders are not the same order: the top tier is
# the most capable alias AND roughly twice the price of the one below it, and
# it consumes the subscription quota much faster.
#
#   The normal ceiling for CRITICAL work is the tier named here.
#   The tier(s) ABOVE it are for SUPER-CRITICAL work and extreme ONE-OFF cases.
#
# WHAT THIS DECLARATION FEEDS: guard-model-ceiling.sh (PreToolUse[Agent])
# REFUSES any spawn whose resolved model sits on a HIGHER tier than this one,
# unless the prompt carries a live
#     model-ceiling-ack: <reason>
# line (logged to .claude/state/model-ceiling-acks.log; a bare marker, and a
# reason that is nothing but an assertion of merit, exempt nothing). At or
# below the ceiling the guard is SILENT. It compares TIER RANKS and never an
# alias by name, so a model added above the current top needs a re-derived
# MODEL_TIERS line and no change to any guard.
#
# BLANK OR ABSENT = NO CEILING, ANNOUNCED. The guard then allows every spawn
# and says loudly, per spawn, that nothing is checking — never silently. That
# is deliberate: this engine has twice shipped a green check over a defense
# that was not running, and an undeclared ceiling that looked enforced would be
# the third. Delete this line and enforcement stops with a noise, not quietly.
#
# WHY A KEY AND NOT PROSE: it was prose, for exactly one day. The founder's
# ruling of 2026-09-03 was recorded in CLAUDE.md and as a comment here, with
# "no guard reads this today" stated in it, and the measurement this repository
# keeps about its own rules is that a rule left as prose gets broken while every
# rule with a guard behind it holds.
MODEL_CEILING="opus"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QUOTA_PAUSE_PERCENT  (used by: scripts/quota-watch.sh and its SessionStart
#                       notice, scripts/hooks/session-start-quota.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUNDER'S QUOTA RULE, AS DATA. His words, 2026-09-10 (ruling §87):
#
#   "quota polling: every 5 minutes from now. And once it crosses the 93%
#    threshold: PAUSE subagents. Then resume after quota rest."
#
# HIS UPDATE, 2026-09-25 (ruling §87): at or above this percentage, do not
# pause when the reset is LESS than 20 minutes away (exactly 20 still
# pauses), and a hold already in place releases inside that window, keeping
# the same agent. Polling stays every 5 minutes at every usage level: "Well,
# I've changed my mind. Let's drop the 30-minute check nonsense. Keep it
# consistent at 5 minutes."
#
# The percentage lives here, once, the way MODEL_CEILING does, for a session
# that governs this engine itself. An adopter declares its own line in its own
# orchestration.config. The watcher reads the five-hour window from Claude
# Code's own get_usage control request at every poll (falling back to
# ~/.claude/statusline-payload.json), polls every 300 s, and wakes the lead at
# this percentage (never inside the last 20 minutes), when the reset is less
# than 20 minutes away with agents held, and at the reset; the lead pauses
# and resumes.
#
# BLANK OR ABSENT = THE WATCHER CANNOT RUN, ANNOUNCED. There is no built-in
# fallback value.
QUOTA_PAUSE_PERCENT=93

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECOND_REVIEW_REPOS  (used by: scripts/review-watch.sh, the plugin monitor
#                       `review-watch`; scripts/operator-fences.sh install,
#                       which copies it into each fence launcher, read there
#                       by the Git fence and by `workspaces.sh merge`)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH REPOSITORIES GET THE AUTOMATIC SECOND REVIEW. Ruling §113, 2026-10-08:
# "A regular RichOS user can never be expected anything even remotely close to
# that. So, this all must be completely automated." The watcher starts a review
# by a different model of a teammate's own commits at its handover, every hour
# of a long job, when it goes quiet, and at each Codex READY entry, in these
# repositories only; record-only repositories are left out (richos-hq plan
# 2026-10-09 §6). The value is the one bash sets when it sources this file
# (scripts/lib/operator_fences.py review_repos asks bash; it parses no lines):
# the main checkout's folder names (letters, digits, '.', '_', '-') separated
# by spaces. A value that is not names, or a file bash cannot source cleanly
# (a syntax error, a last command that fails), refuses install and every land.
#
# AND NO WORK LANDS IN THEM WITHOUT A PASSING REVIEW OF EXACTLY ITS TIP:
# `workspaces.sh merge` refuses first, and the Git fence refuses every other
# move of main (a plain merge, a fast-forward, a codex/ branch's land), with
# the fence on. An edit here reaches the fence only through
# `operator-fences.sh install`; `operator-fences.sh status` reports a launcher
# that disagrees.
#
# BLANK OR ABSENT = NO REVIEW STARTS BY ITSELF, ANNOUNCED once per session.
SECOND_REVIEW_REPOS="richos"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENTRYPOINTS  (used by: scripts/entrypoint-currency-lint.sh,
#               contract-integrity-probe.sh Layer EP; parsed ONLY through
#               scripts/lib/entrypoints.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH COMMAND IS THE CURRENT ONE, AS DATA — and what it REPLACED.
#
# Records separated by `;`. Three fields per record, separated by `|`:
#
#     <task a person is trying to do> | <the command today> | <what it replaced>
#
# The third field may be empty (a task whose command never had a predecessor);
# it may name several paths. Paths are repo-relative and are matched by
# BASENAME, so an instruction naming the same script through $ENGINE_ROOT, a
# tilde or a bare relative path is the same instruction.
#
# WHAT THIS DECLARATION FEEDS: `scripts/entrypoint-currency-lint.sh` refuses a
# STANDING INSTRUCTION (see INSTRUCTION_SURFACES below) that names a superseded
# entrypoint while never naming the one that replaced it, and Layer EP of the
# integrity probe runs that lint plus a two-sided canary. A line carrying
#     entrypoint-exempt: <reason>
# is not checked — for a migration note, a history section or a test that
# asserts the old path still works. A bare marker exempts nothing.
#
# IT GOVERNS WHAT WE TELL PEOPLE, NEVER WHAT IS ALLOWED. Every superseded path
# here still runs, still has its tests, and is in several cases CALLED by the
# canonical one — spawn.sh creates its workspace through
# create-teammate-worktree.sh and builds its payload through
# prepare-agent-spawn.py. Breaking the old path would break the new one.
#
# WHY IT IS A KEY AND NOT A HABIT, dated and costed: `scripts/spawn.sh` landed
# 2026-09-13 at 23:24 — one command, every PreToolUse[Agent] guard from every
# surface evaluated before anything is created. The NEXT session did not use it,
# because scripts/hooks/engine-status.sh — the SessionStart announcement every
# session boots with — still named the four-step path it replaced. That session
# paid 130 s and two sequential guard refusals for work that takes 1.3 s through
# spawn.sh, and reported success, because it did exactly what it was told. Both
# paths worked, so nothing went red and no test failed. The founder found it by
# paying the old cost and recognizing the number. Failure type V,
# richos-hq/docs/verification/lifecycle-failure-record-2026-09-13.md §10f.
#
# ADD A ROW THE DAY A MECHANISM IS REPLACED, in the same commit that replaces
# it. A row that is currently green still earns its place: it is what refuses
# the reintroduction of a retired path into an instruction two months from now.
ENTRYPOINTS="starting a teammate | scripts/spawn.sh | scripts/prepare-agent-spawn.py mega-lander/create-teammate-worktree.sh; deleting an agent workspace | mega-lander/workspaces.sh | scripts/remove-agent-worktree.sh scripts/reap-agent-worktrees.sh"

# INSTRUCTION_SURFACES  (used by: scripts/entrypoint-currency-lint.sh,
#                        contract-integrity-probe.sh Layer EP)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILES THAT TELL SOMEBODY WHAT TO DO. Space-separated glob patterns,
# relative to the repository root. Only these are checked, and the narrowness is
# the design rather than a shortcut: a reference table, a measurement, an
# architecture note and a failure record all name superseded commands
# LEGITIMATELY and constantly, and a check that swept them would be waived on
# the day it landed. What made type V expensive was one narrow class — the
# instruction that ARRIVES BEFORE ANY WORK and is read as current by
# construction.
#
# A pattern that matches nothing is silent, so one list serves the engine and
# every governed repository: the engine's own run covers engine-status.sh, a
# product repository's run covers its CLAUDE.md, its skills and its agent
# definitions.
#
# ADD A SURFACE the moment something new starts issuing standing instructions.
# Do NOT add README.md, docs/ or the record: they DESCRIBE, and description that
# names an old command is how a project remembers what it did.
INSTRUCTION_SURFACES="CLAUDE.md AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md.template .claude/agents/*.md skills/*/SKILL.md skills/*/*/SKILL.md scripts/hooks/engine-status.sh"

# READER_TEAMMATE / READER_SIGNAL_SOURCE_DIRS were REMOVED on 2026-09-14 with
# their only consumer, scripts/hooks/reader-teammate-hint.sh. That hook fired
# only on a GENERIC subagent_type, and clause 5 of guard-worktree-isolation.sh
# — first in the same PreToolUse[Agent] chain — already exits 2 on exactly that
# condition, so the hint was unreachable except behind an accepted
# `generic-agent:` hatch (one use, ever). Staffing is the lead's judgment and is
# not an invariant of any artifact; this engine no longer spends a blocking hook
# on every spawn to express a routing preference. See
# docs/verification/verification-layer-design-2026-09-14.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CREATOR_TEAMMATE  (used by: verify-agent-prompt.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hiring/creation meta-role. The agent-not-found check (which rejects a
# prompt referencing a .claude/agents/<name>.md that does not exist) is skipped
# for this role, because its whole job is to CREATE that definition file. Ships
# as "dean" (the engine's HR/hiring meta-role).
CREATOR_TEAMMATE="dean"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Artifact collection  (used by: scripts/collect-worktree-artifacts.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gitignored test/QA evidence directories to mirror out of a teammate worktree
# into the main checkout before the worktree is removed. Paths are relative to
# a checkout root; include any per-source-tree variants (e.g. "app/test-results").
#   ARTIFACT_MERGE_DIRS   — merged newest-wins, other files preserved.
#   ARTIFACT_REPLACE_DIRS — replaced wholesale (per-run aggregates).
# NOTE: this is distinct from qa-audits/ and ui-ux-signoffs/ (committed QA/design
# deliverables that ride the normal git merge — never gitignored, never listed here).
ARTIFACT_MERGE_DIRS="test-results output"
ARTIFACT_REPLACE_DIRS="playwright-report"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# QA install-fresh dispatch gate  (used by: verify-agent-prompt.sh)  — OPT-IN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OFF by default. This gate is part of the ADVANCED "identity-or-refuse" tier
# (see reference/advanced-tier/). Turn it ON only if you have adopted a
# device/install-fresh pipeline: it then blocks a spawn that will RUN your app
# unless the prompt cites an install-fresh script as a precondition, or carries
# an auditable `data-contract-bypass:` opt-out line. Leave it 0 unless the
# reference tier applies to your stack.
#
# RE-SPECIFIED 2026-09-15. It used to fire on a QA verb plus a platform noun,
# which is ordinary engineering prose: replayed over 968 real spawn prompts it
# fired 78 times, the citation path was taken 0 times, and every single fire
# was waived or refused — 486 waivers in its ledger, the largest in the engine.
# A gate whose escape hatch is its only exit is charging a toll, and habitual
# waiving is how a guard dies. It now needs REACHABILITY (the dispatch's
# workspace is in this repository, not another one) and EVIDENCE (APP_RUNTIME_RE
# or QA_ROLE_AGENTS) on top of the two regexes, which is a strict narrowing:
# it cannot fire where it was silent before.
ENABLE_QA_INSTALL_FRESH_GATE=0

# The following are consulted ONLY when ENABLE_QA_INSTALL_FRESH_GATE=1.
# INSTALL_FRESH_SCRIPTS: space-separated filenames a qualifying prompt must
#   cite as its precondition.
INSTALL_FRESH_SCRIPTS="android-install-fresh.sh ios-install-fresh.sh"
# QA_TRIGGER_RE: extended-regex (case-insensitive) marking a prompt as a
#   test/audit/render/capture task.
QA_TRIGGER_RE='(\btest\b|\baudit\b|\bauditing\b|\btesting\b|\bQA\b|\bverify\b|\bverifies\b|\bverifying\b|\bparity\b|\brender\b|\brenders\b|\brendering\b|\bcapture\b|\bcaptures\b|\bcapturing\b|\bscreenshot\b|\bscreencap\b|install-fresh)'
# LOCAL_APP_CONTEXT_RE: extended-regex (case-sensitive) marking the task as
#   operating against the LOCAL APP (as opposed to app-free work). Customize
#   these tokens to your platform (device names, build markers, screen names).
LOCAL_APP_CONTEXT_RE='(install-fresh|emulator|simulator|the app|local app|BUILD_SHA|device[[:space:]]+QA|home[ -]?state)'
# APP_RUNTIME_RE: extended-regex (case-sensitive) naming evidence that the
#   dispatch will EXERCISE a built app — a device tool, a packaged artifact, a
#   build marker, a screen class. NAMING A PLATFORM IS NOT EXERCISING AN APP,
#   and that distinction is the whole of the 2026-09-15 re-specification: the
#   gate fired 78 times over 968 real spawn prompts and not one of the 78 was
#   app work, because the two regexes above match ordinary engineering prose.
#   Keep this list SHORT and specific to things that only appear when an app is
#   actually being run. Widening it is how the gate drifts back to charging a
#   toll on every dispatch.
APP_RUNTIME_RE='(\badb\b|simctl|xcrun|xcodebuild|\./gradlew|[Ee]mulator|[Ss]imulator|\.apk\b|\.ipa\b|BUILD_SHA|client-data-check|MainActivity|HomeView|HomeScreen|Health[[:space:]]*Connect|HealthKit|on[- ]device|physical device|installed[[:space:]]+app|home[ -]?state)'
# QA_ROLE_AGENTS: space-separated subagent types whose JOB is the app (native
#   and visual QA, the client engineers). For these, naming any app surface is
#   evidence enough — they do not get the benefit of the runtime test, because
#   a brief that says only "audit the Home screen and report what you see" is a
#   render task no matter which words it avoids. Empty = the clause is off.
QA_ROLE_AGENTS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The QA walk toolkit  (used by: scripts/lib/qa-toolkit.py, through spawn.sh;
#                                scripts/lib/qa-throwaways.py)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CEO, 2026-09-20: "what else must be done to ensure the QA toolkit
# actually gets used?" The toolkit replaced 111 throwaway helpers across eight
# walks; its README's rule is that a QA brief names the tool from it. That rule
# fails in one way only — the brief does not, because whoever wrote it did not
# have the list in front of them. So spawn.sh appends the README's own table to
# a QA-type teammate's payload, read from the governed repository at spawn time.
#
# QA_TOOLKIT_AGENTS: space-separated subagent types that get the table. The
#   dispatch's TYPE decides, never its prose.
#
#   NOT the same list as QA_ROLE_AGENTS above, deliberately. That one answers
#   "whose job IS the app" for the data-contract gate, and femcboost's copy
#   therefore includes the client engineers, who build the app and do not walk
#   it with a frame reader and an OCR gate. One list answering two questions is
#   how a list stops meaning anything — the reasoning spawn-guard-audience.
#   declaration was written out of. Widen this one here, where its reason is.
QA_TOOLKIT_AGENTS="ray urban kai quint"
# QA_TOOLKIT_DIR: where the committed toolkit lives, relative to the governed
#   repository's root. Its README is the index that travels with the dispatch,
#   and a path under a directory of this name is an ADDITION to the toolkit
#   rather than a throwaway when the land counts what a walk wrote.
QA_TOOLKIT_DIR="richos/app/scripts/qa"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets scanner  (used by: scripts/hooks/scan-secrets.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Blocks a Write/Edit/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit whose new content looks like a
# live secret (vendor-prefixed API keys, PEM private-key blocks, or a
# high-entropy password=/api_key=/secret=/token= literal). Answers the "will
# these autonomous agents leak my credentials" objection structurally.
#
# SECRET_SCAN_MIN_LENGTH / SECRET_SCAN_MIN_ENTROPY gate the GENERIC
# key=value-literal detector only (the vendor-prefix patterns — AWS/GitHub/
# Anthropic/OpenAI/Stripe/PEM — are already narrow enough by their fixed
# prefix that they don't need an entropy gate). Raise SECRET_SCAN_MIN_ENTROPY
# if your codebase's legitimate high-entropy strings (hashes, generated IDs)
# are tripping false positives; lower SECRET_SCAN_MIN_LENGTH/ENTROPY if you
# want stricter catching of short secrets at the cost of more false positives.
SECRET_SCAN_MIN_LENGTH=12
SECRET_SCAN_MIN_ENTROPY="3.0"

# SECRET_SCAN_ALLOWLIST: space-separated literal substrings that are NEVER
# flagged, even if they would otherwise match a pattern above — for your own
# known-safe fixtures/placeholders beyond what the scanner already exempts by
# construction (a repeated-character run, a recognized placeholder word, or
# ${VAR}/process.env/os.environ syntax — see scan-secrets.sh's header for the
# full list; a placeholder like `re_xxxxxxxxx` is already exempt via the
# repeated-character rule and needs no entry here). Leave blank if you have
# no additional fixtures to exempt.
SECRET_SCAN_ALLOWLIST=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BAN_WORKFLOW_TOOL  (used by: scripts/hooks/guard-workflow-ban.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 (or unset)  the `Workflow` orchestration tool is BLOCKED in this repository
# 0             the tool is permitted, and the guard says so on stderr the
#               moment a Workflow call arrives — never a silent permission
#
# DEFAULT-ON ON PURPOSE. `/workflow` has failed in production sessions and cost
# an operator real time; orchestration happens via individual `Agent`
# dispatches. A ban that defaults OFF is a ban that disappears the first time
# somebody adopts the engine without reading this file, so the only way to
# permit the tool is to write the word down here, where a reviewer sees it.
# The guard still stands down entirely in a repository that never adopted the
# engine — a plugin enabled at user scope loads everywhere, and a guard must
# not govern a repo that never invited it.
BAN_WORKFLOW_TOOL=1

# SECRET_SCAN_CODE_AWARE: 0 (default) or 1. OPT-IN precision for the GENERIC
# key=value detector only — the vendor-prefix and PEM patterns never consult it.
# With 1, a value that is plainly CODE (structural punctuation ( ) [ ] < > ? ! { }
# or a dotted member path — `token: NSObjectProtocol?`, `config.creds.apiKey`)
# or plainly DESCRIPTIVE (every -/_ segment purely alphabetic or purely numeric —
# "test-password", "BEARER_PROTOCOL_FUTURE_TOKEN") is not flagged.
#
# IT IS NOT FREE, and the cost is measured rather than guessed: it also waves
# through an all-alpha passphrase, an all-alpha random token, an all-digit long
# value, and an alpha-dash-alpha token that the strict default catches. Both the
# benefit and the cost are pinned as named cases in scan-secrets.test.sh. Default
# OFF so nobody's security posture changes by adopting the engine; turn it on
# when fixture/test-literal noise is costing more than that risk.
SECRET_SCAN_CODE_AWARE=0

# INFLIGHT_ACK_TIMEOUT_MIN: minutes. How long after a "main moved under you"
# notice the operator is told, at the end of a turn, that no acknowledgement has
# arrived and the §8b fallback (TaskStop + respawn) is now the move.
#
# Default 30, and it is measured rather than picked: on the machine this was
# built on, 22 completed worker run segments ran p50 2375s (40m) and p90 4258s
# (71m). A queued message is delivered at the receiver's next TOOL ROUND, which
# is bounded by one tool call rather than a whole segment, so 30 minutes spans
# many delivery opportunities for a teammate that is running at all — while
# staying below the median segment, so the notice arrives while the teammate is
# still inside a segment it can act in. Nothing is ever killed automatically.
#
# Raise it if your teammates run longer tool calls; lower it if a stale land
# costs more than a premature nag.
INFLIGHT_ACK_TIMEOUT_MIN=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dialect guard  (used by: scripts/hooks/dialect-guard.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIALECT_TARGET: the dialect every word this repository writes must be in.
# BLANK = OFF, silently — a repository that has declared no dialect has made no
# claim, and inventing one for it would be a policy expansion rather than a
# guard.
#
# `en-US` is the only value with a dictionary shipped
# (scripts/lib/dialect-en-US.dict, en-GB -> en-US). Any other value makes the
# guard say out loud that it checked nothing, rather than pretend.
#
# WHY IT SHIPS SET RATHER THAN BLANK: this engine's own record is American
# English by CEO ruling (2026-08-29), and the sample values in this file are
# this project's real values throughout (PROTECTED_PATHS, PUBLICATION_BOUNDARY).
# An adopter whose product speaks another dialect BLANKS THIS LINE — that is
# the whole off switch, and it is one edit in the file adopters already edit.
# Never weaken the dictionary to make a write pass.
DIALECT_TARGET="en-US"

# DIALECT_SCAN_ALLOWLIST: space-separated literal substrings that are never
# flagged. For your own known-safe strings — a third-party protocol value, a
# product name you do not own, a quoted phrase that must stay as written and
# cannot sit in a code span or a blockquote. The guard already exempts fenced
# code, inline code spans, blockquotes, URLs/paths, code identifiers, captured-
# evidence paths and vendor legal files by construction; this is for what is
# left. A line carrying `dialect-exempt: <reason>` is the per-site alternative.
DIALECT_SCAN_ALLOWLIST=""

# DIALECT_EXEMPT_PATHS: space-separated path substrings whose writes are never
# checked, on top of the built-in captured-evidence set (raw/, cold-open/,
# transcripts/, fixtures/, corpora/, snapshots/, logs/, node_modules/, vendor/,
# third_party/, *.log, *.jsonl, lockfiles, LICENSE/NOTICE/COPYING).
DIALECT_EXEMPT_PATHS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STANDING OWNERSHIP  (used by: scripts/hooks/guard-owned-state.sh,
#                               scripts/owned-state.sh,
#                               scripts/lib/owned-systems.py)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every other mechanism in this engine is triggered by an ACT — a spawn, a
# land, a commit, a turn ending. This one is triggered by nothing at all, which
# is the point: on 2026-09-10 the founder found, himself, a workflow red on
# main for thirteen days, 30 GB of worktrees nothing would reclaim, and the
# engine's own hook text on his screen. NONE OF IT WAS ASSIGNED, and the
# worktree state was already printed in that session's start-up notice and was
# read past. Surfacing had been tried and had failed that morning.
#
# OWNED_SYSTEMS_DECLARATION names the file that lists what the orchestrator
# owns the health of, each row with a machine-checkable definition of healthy
# and the command that decides it. Relative paths are resolved against the
# governed repository first, then the engine — so an adopter may ship its own
# file at this path to extend or replace the engine's rows, and a row whose
# `jurisdiction:` line does not hold in a repository is silent there.
OWNED_SYSTEMS_DECLARATION="owned-systems.declaration"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPAWN_GUARD_AUDIENCE_DECLARATION
#   (used by: scripts/lib/spawn-guard-audience.py and, through it,
#             scripts/lib/spawn.py, scripts/app-engine-hook.py and the RichOS
#             app's EngineProfile)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHOSE WORK DOES EACH PreToolUse[Agent] GUARD'S REASON PROTECT? The RichOS
# desktop app dispatches background work on a non-technical user's own Mac, and
# the CEO's §57 is that nothing the user runs depends on our operator setup. So
# the app's own prepare/spawn path is judged ONLY by guards whose reason
# protects that user's own work — never by a guard whose reason is the
# development session's paused CI, unasked decision, stale staging or
# design-round convention.
#
# Which guard falls on which side is a DECLARED LIST, not an inference: it was
# an inference until 2026-09-18, typed identically into two files, and one of
# the two entries was an operator-session guard judging a user's assignment.
# The file named here carries every guard, its side, the one-sentence reason
# for that side, and — on the user-work side — the app's own words for a
# refusal the user can still see. Relative paths resolve against the governed
# repository first, then the engine.
SPAWN_GUARD_AUDIENCE_DECLARATION="spawn-guard-audience.declaration"

# OWNED_STATE_GATE — the OFF switch for the blocking gate, not an on switch.
# Absent or truthy: guard-owned-state.sh refuses ONE dispatch per session while
# the oldest standing system has had nothing done about it, and is silent for
# the rest of that session once anything has. Set to 0/off/false/no to stand it
# down entirely, in which case scripts/owned-state.sh still reports by hand.
#
# ABSENT MEANS ENABLED, deliberately, and unlike STAGING_TREES's adoption
# switch: staging is a thing most repositories genuinely do not have, while
# "some system whose health you own is bad and nobody did anything" is true of
# every repository an orchestrator runs in. Enforcement that must be switched
# on is enforcement a fresh clone does not have.
OWNED_STATE_GATE="1"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAND_COMPLETENESS_ENFORCE / CHECK_LAND_COMPLETENESS
#          (used by: scripts/hooks/guard-ci-red-lands.sh section 2b,
#                    scripts/lib/land-residue-gate.py,
#                    scripts/land-completeness.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A land ends with nothing left behind that a person or a machine can mistake
# for live work. On 2026-09-10 every land step carrying a gate was satisfied on
# every land, and the one step with no gate — removing the landed agent's
# worktree — was skipped ten-plus times in a row, until 25 worktrees and 25
# branches were registered and the founder's terminal showed a row per worktree,
# each frozen on that agent's last action with the clock still running.
#
# CHECK_LAND_COMPLETENESS  1 (or absent) runs the check at every land; 0 stands
#                          it down entirely. Absent means ON, because an
#                          incomplete land is a state every repository this
#                          orchestration runs in can reach.
#
# LAND_COMPLETENESS_ENFORCE  0 (the default, and it ships this way) ANNOUNCES an
#                          incomplete land and allows it. 1 REFUSES the next
#                          land until the previous one is finished, or until the
#                          retention is declared with `land-residue-ack:`.
#
# IT SHIPS AT 0 BY REQUIREMENT, NOT BY CAUTION. The plan's G4 keeps installation
# approval with the founder: he sees the measured rate and the acknowledgement's
# shape BEFORE anything blocks, and there is no implicit approval from a merge
# or from silence. The rate he is being shown, re-derivable with
# `scripts/land-completeness-measure.py` over every orchestrator transcript on
# this machine and scoped to the ledger epoch: 17.7% of lands would be refused,
# 7.6% would be refused by a naive version and are exempted because their owner
# held a running lock, and 61% are undecidable and are never refused at all.
#
# Flipping this to 1 is one committed, diffable line, which is the whole reason
# it is a config key and not an environment variable somebody exports.
CHECK_LAND_COMPLETENESS="1"
LAND_COMPLETENESS_ENFORCE="1"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE HE LEFT OFF — the return report (scripts/hooks/left-off-report.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When a person comes back after a break, the FIRST thing in front of the
# assistant is his own last pre-gap message, verbatim, plus what ran across the
# gap and what GIT says happened to it. It refuses nothing; there is no exit
# code to clear and no acknowledgement line.
#
# CHECK_LEFT_OFF=0 stands it down, and the stand-down is ANNOUNCED on both
# channels rather than being silent.
#
# LEFT_OFF_GAP_MINUTES is what counts as "away". 120 is measured, not chosen:
# over the 42 transcripts on this machine that hold two or more of his typed
# messages, 1946 intervals between them,
#
#     >=  45 min   202  (10.4%)      >= 120 min   104  (5.3%, 2.48 per session)
#     >=  90 min   131  ( 6.7%)      >= 180 min    79  (4.1%)
#
#     $ python3 ... over ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl, origin.kind=human
#
# so at 120 the report speaks about twice a session and a coffee break is not a
# return. The gap it was written for was 9h 01m.
CHECK_LEFT_OFF="1"
LEFT_OFF_GAP_MINUTES="120"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FAILURE TYPE — "type" and "failure" together (scripts/hooks/failure-type-lookup.sh,
#                scripts/hooks/guard-failure-type-answer.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The operator's rule: whenever he mentions the words "type" and "failure" close
# to each other, the answer comes from the failure register by number
# (`Type <N>: <heading>`, verbatim), and the instance is committed to the
# register in the same turn. The lookup hook hands the lead the register's live
# type list; the Stop gate refuses the turn end until the register was read, a
# type was named from it, and the register has a commit made during the turn.
#
# FAILURE_TYPE_REGISTER is the register's path, absolute or relative to the
# entity root. EMPTY MEANS NOT CONFIGURED: both hooks are silent on ordinary
# messages and SAY SO when his message carries the words. It is empty here
# because this repository holds no register; the entity that keeps one declares
# it in its own orchestration.config.
#
# CHECK_FAILURE_TYPE=0 stands both down, announced, never silent.
CHECK_FAILURE_TYPE="1"
FAILURE_TYPE_REGISTER=""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESOURCE WAITS — no job waits in line (scripts/hooks/guard-resource-waits.sh,
#                  scripts/lib/resource_waits.py)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CEO, 2026-09-25: "A free Mac is used whole; nothing waits in a line."
# The lead's turn end is REFUSED while any job has waited more than
# RESOURCE_WAIT_MINUTES on a shared resource: the test VM (a slot-queue caller,
# or a go-file wait), CPU admission (reserve.py, proof-run.py), or a wait an
# escalation reports. The refusal names the waiter, the resource, the holder and
# whether the holder is using it right now. Only the wait ending clears it; an
# acknowledgement never does.
#
# RESOURCE_WAIT_MINUTES is the CEO's ten minutes. CHECK_RESOURCE_WAITS=0 stands
# the gate down, announced to the operator every time, never silent.
CHECK_RESOURCE_WAITS="1"
RESOURCE_WAIT_MINUTES="10"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCRATCH_* — the scratch reaper (scripts/scratch-reaper.sh,
#             scripts/hooks/session-start-scratch.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On 2026-09-17 macOS said "Your disk is almost full": 1.8 GB free of 460 GB.
# 19 GB of it was scratch nobody was using, and it came back only because a
# person deleted it by hand after a warning. The CEO's words that morning:
# "There needs to be a mechanism to make sure that scratch directories get
# deleted on a regular basis. I don't want piles of garbage to pile up
# endlessly." (ceo-decisions §46.)
#
# EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS MEASURED ON THIS MACHINE AND CARRIES ITS MEASUREMENT.
# None is a tool's default, and none was picked because it looked round — the
# standing rule is that a third-party default is never adopted for anything
# unless it is proven to be the best value, and a value nobody can defend is a
# value nobody can argue with on the day it turns out to be wrong.
#
# SCRATCH_REAPER_ENABLE  1 (or absent) arms the reaper and the SessionStart
#                        notice; 0 stands both down. Absent means ON: every
#                        machine that runs agents accumulates dead scratch.
SCRATCH_REAPER_ENABLE="1"

# SCRATCH_CLAUDE_ROOTS — the scratch roots to sweep, space-separated. `%u` is
# replaced with the running user's numeric uid. Claude Code writes
# <root>/<slugified-cwd>/<session-uuid>/{scratchpad,tasks}; the reaper reasons
# about the SESSION-UUID level and never about the root itself.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-17 09:2xZ under /private/tmp/claude-501: 68 slug
# directories, 5.2 GB, of which 4.27 GB belongs to the ONE session that was
# running at the time and is never a candidate. /tmp is listed as well as
# /private/tmp because on macOS they are the same directory reached by two
# names, and a host that resolves only one of them is not a host to guess at.
SCRATCH_CLAUDE_ROOTS="/private/tmp/claude-%u /tmp/claude-%u"

# Named scratch is resolved by scripts/lib/machine_settings.py and collected
# only by the landed agent's scoped sweep. Scheduled sweeps leave it alone.

# SCRATCH_KEPT_DIR / SCRATCH_KEPT_RETENTION_DAYS — where an automatic land
# (mega-lander/workspaces.py, _keep_ignored) moves a merged, ended workspace's
# ignored files instead of waiting for a waiver, and how many days each
# <key>-<time> directory there is kept before the scheduled sweep deletes it.
# The path is the workspace registry's own (state_dir() + /kept). Before these
# keys nothing ever removed them (§54). 7 days: the retention the campaign
# roots above already use for "a week after it goes quiet".
SCRATCH_KEPT_DIR="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/state/workspaces/kept"
SCRATCH_KEPT_RETENTION_DAYS="7"

# SCRATCH_BUILD_CACHE_* / SCRATCH_CARGO_TARGET_ROOTS — build output on the
# external drive (scripts/lib/build_caches.py, 2026-10-08). The drive was 98%
# full three weeks after it was added, 824 GB of it in caches/ that nothing
# ever removed: every checkout's own Cargo and native-app folders after the
# checkout was gone, and every Cargo dependency unit after no build needed it.
# NO SIZE CAP: what stays is what is in use or was used recently (CEO
# 2026-10-08: "there's no need for any unreasonable limits as long as the
# auto-clean-up is working").
#   Cache paths come from machine settings; live checkouts from Git and the
#   existing workspace records. Unreadable ownership never permits deletion.
#   ORPHAN_HOURS 6    an orphan is kept while anything inside it was written
#                     or read this recently: a copy of a checkout outside git,
#                     or a test fixture, still building.
#   IDLE_HOURS 72     a Cargo unit no build has read for three days is deleted.
#                     Measured on 2026-10-08: 2,456 of 4,686 units in the
#                     shared build directory had not been read for 3 days, and
#                     a unit that is needed again is rebuilt by Cargo.
SCRATCH_BUILD_CACHE_ORPHAN_HOURS="6"
SCRATCH_BUILD_CACHE_IDLE_HOURS="72"

# SCRATCH_TMP_PATTERNS — glob patterns, relative to $TMPDIR, for the temporary
# workspaces test harnesses create and are supposed to remove. A directory is
# a candidate ONLY if it matches one of these AND no running process holds it
# open (read from lsof; if lsof cannot run the answer is INDETERMINATE and
# nothing is deleted).
#
# THAT MEASUREMENT WAS TAKEN AND THE SENTENCE IT PRODUCED WAS WRONG WITHIN
# TWELVE HOURS. It used to read: "MEASURED 2026-09-17: ZERO survivors under
# $TMPDIR — the harnesses do clean them up... This pattern list is therefore a
# live no-op today, and is declared anyway because the day a harness is killed
# mid-run is the day it stops being one."
#
# That day was THE SAME DAY. At 22:39 on 2026-09-17 root-contract.mutation.sh
# was killed mid-run and left 105.3 GB in ONE directory named
# `root-mutation.XXXXXX.w21XvsYsCt`. By morning $TMPDIR held 114.5 GB in
# 88,829 entries and the Data volume was down to 49 GB free.
#
# THE REAPER RAN TWICE ACROSS THAT WINDOW AND NEVER SAW IT. 21:40 before,
# 03:40 after — the 03:40 run freed 156 KB — and the string "root-mutation"
# appears nowhere in ~/.claude/state/scratch-reaper.log on any run, ever,
# because `root-mutation.*` matched neither glob above and scan_tmp() skips
# what does not match.
#
# THE LESSON IS ABOUT THE SHAPE, NOT THE GLOBS. An allowlist of names can only
# ever contain the names somebody thought of, and the directory that filled the
# disk was named by a bare `mktemp` in a file nobody was thinking about. Adding
# `root-mutation.*` here would fix the 2026-09-17 incident and nothing else.
# So the coverage is now in TWO parts and this list is the smaller one:
#
#   SCRATCH_ROOT_NAME below — the allocator's root, swept DENY-BY-DEFAULT.
#     Anything under it with no live owner goes, whatever it is called. New
#     code allocates there and needs no declaration here, ever.
#   SCRATCH_LEGACY_TMP_PATTERNS below — the names that already exist in the
#     wild, swept for one release cycle while callers migrate to the allocator.
#
# This list keeps its original meaning: harness workspaces that are NOT under
# the allocator root and are not legacy families. It stays because removing a
# working declaration to prove a point is how coverage gets lost.
SCRATCH_TMP_PATTERNS="richos-*-workspace richos-work-*"

# SCRATCH_ROOT_NAME — the single directory under $TMPDIR that
# scripts/lib/scratch.sh allocates into, and that the reaper sweeps
# DENY-BY-DEFAULT: every child is a candidate unless a live owner is proven.
#
# A FIXED NAME, NOT A PATTERN, and that is the whole point of it. There is no
# glob to widen and nothing to predict, because the only way to get a directory
# under this root is to ask scratch.sh for one — so a child with no ledger row
# is garbage by construction rather than by guess. The 105 GB escaped an
# allowlist; it could not have escaped this.
SCRATCH_ROOT_NAME="richos-scratch"

# SCRATCH_DEFAULT_TTL_MINUTES — how long an allocation is assumed to be wanted
# when its caller does not say. 360 = 6 hours.
#
# MEASURED: the longest legitimate run this engine has on record is a full
# concurrent mutation pass at 178 and 181 minutes (two of them, 2026-09-08,
# which is why a full engine suite is never briefed). 360 is twice the longest
# thing anybody has actually run, so a TTL expiring under a live process is not
# a case that happens in practice — and when it does happen the LIVE OWNER
# STILL WINS, because TTL alone never authorises a deletion. See the note on
# scan_scratch_root: a live pid is always KEEP.
SCRATCH_DEFAULT_TTL_MINUTES="360"

# SCRATCH_MIN_OWNER_PID — the lowest pid that may be believed as an allocation's
# owner WHEN THE PID CAME OFF THE DIRECTORY NAME rather than out of the ledger.
#
# frank-opus-garbage1's D11: he created `1-frank-immortal-b` in the allocator root
# and the reaper answered "pid 1 is ALIVE (owner of 'unrecorded', from the
# directory name) — a live owner is kept whatever its TTL says". Forever, at any
# size, with no alert and no TTL escape, AND SILENTLY BY CONSTRUCTION, because a
# KEEP is the reaper working as designed. `pid_alive()` returns True on
# PermissionError, which is what pid 1 gives, and macOS recycles pids at 99998 —
# so a week-old name whose leading digits now match a live pid is not exotic.
#
# 100, MEASURED on this machine 2026-09-18:
#   lowest pid owned by uid 501 (us) : 160
#   pids owned by root               : 1, 88, 90, 92, 93, ...
# A pid below 100 on this machine is a boot daemon and cannot be the process that
# asked the allocator for a directory.
#
# THE FLOOR IS THE WEAKEST OF THE THREE TESTS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT ALONE. A
# name-derived pid must also belong to THIS uid (the allocator runs as us, so a
# process of another user cannot be the caller — that test alone retires pid 1) and
# must have started NO LATER than the directory was created (a process that began
# afterwards cannot have made it, which is what pid reuse looks like). A LEDGER row
# is exempt from all three: it was written by the allocator at allocation time,
# whereas digits in a name can be produced by naming a directory.
SCRATCH_MIN_OWNER_PID="100"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_* — the piles under ~/ab that belong to a finished campaign
# (Frank's D3). MEASURED AND REPORTED, NEVER DELETED AUTOMATICALLY.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# His finding: `richos-rechecks` and `richos-password-free-workspaces` sit under
# ~/ab in no ledger row and under no declared root, so the worktree reaper will
# never see them and the scratch reaper will never see them. Re-measured
# 2026-09-18 at 18.90 GB and 13.75 GB.
#
# HIS PROPOSED SIGNAL — "not in the ledger" — DOES NOT SURVIVE THE CENSUS, and
# this is the measurement that decided the shape. Run over the whole of ~/ab:
#
#      bytes  name                                 age_d  .git?  in-ledger?
#   18.90 GB  richos-rechecks                        1.5    yes      NO
#   13.75 GB  richos-password-free-workspaces        7.7    yes      NO
#    7.55 GB  richos-alex                           14.2    yes      NO
#    1.96 GB  li-profile-data-grabber               33.2    yes      NO   <- Norm's repo
#    1.21 GB  prospects                             10.1    yes      NO   <- Mark's repo
#    0.97 GB  press-and-publicity                    7.7    yes      NO
#    0.85 GB  saferecord                            57.1    yes      NO
#    0.49 GB  richos-cleanup-source                  7.7    yes      NO
#    0.44 GB  fitapp                               176.0    yes      NO   <- the product
#   ... 24 of 29 directories are unnamed, and most of them are HIS PROJECTS.
#
# So under ~/ab the default is "this is somebody's work", which is the exact
# opposite of the default under $TMPDIR. The deny-by-default shape that is right
# there would nominate `fitapp` and `prospects` here.
#
# THE ASYMMETRY RUNS THE OTHER WAY, SO THE SHAPE DOES TOO. An enumeration is
# normally the wrong answer — it is the whole D1 lesson — but the failure modes are
# not comparable. A missed campaign root costs disk space that the watchdog's ~/ab
# consumer line still reports; a deny-by-default miss costs the product tree. AN
# ENUMERATION THAT CAN ONLY FAIL TO NOMINATE IS A SAFE ENUMERATION.
#
# AND NOTHING HERE IS EVER DELETED AUTOMATICALLY. Every one of these trees contains
# a `.git`, which is wall 2 — "a scratch directory holding a checkout is somebody's
# work" — and this program refuses those everywhere else. §54 has a second branch
# for exactly this: "if the clean-up fails or impossible for some reason, then Rich
# must get a MASSIVE ALERT about it and get on with manually deleting the garbage".
# A campaign root past its retention is counted into `skipped`, which is what the
# garbage alarm reads, and the reason carries the `rm -rf`.

# The operator may set RICHOS_CAMPAIGN_PARENT in private machine settings.
# Empty nominates no historical campaign directories on a fresh installation.
SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_PARENT=""

# SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_ROOTS — glob patterns, relative to the parent above. ONLY names
# whose throwaway nature is EVIDENCED. `richos-alex` (7.55 GB, 14.2 d) is
# deliberately NOT here: its name suggests a personal checkout and nothing
# establishes that it is finished with, so it is the CEO's word to give and not
# mine to assume. Naming it would be the kind of confident guess this whole record
# exists to avoid.
SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_ROOTS="richos-rechecks richos-password-free-workspaces richos-cleanup-source richos-recovery-codex"

# SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_RETENTION_DAYS — how long after its last touch a campaign root
# is reported. 7 days.
#
# WHAT IT DOES ON ITS FIRST RUN — read off the shipped code, not predicted:
#
#   $ scripts/scratch-reaper.sh --json
#   REPORTED  richos-password-free-workspaces   13.75 GB    7 d untouched
#   REPORTED  richos-recovery-codex              0.00 GB   12 d untouched
#   KEPT      richos-rechecks                   18.90 GB    1 d — inside retention
#   KEPT      richos-cleanup-source              0.49 GB    1 d — inside retention
#
# AND THE TWO "1 d" ROWS ARE WHY THIS BLOCK QUOTES THE PROGRAM RATHER THAN A
# PREDICTION. `stat` on the directories says 7.7 and 1.5 days, and the first draft
# of this comment said the same. The reaper takes its age from THE NEWEST MTIME
# ANYWHERE IN THE TREE, which is the right semantics — "nothing has touched this"
# is a statement about abandonment rather than about the directory inode — and
# something inside `richos-cleanup-source` was written yesterday. The root's mtime
# and the tree's newest mtime are two different facts, and only one of them is the
# one the rule uses.
#
# A week is long enough that a campaign somebody is still reading through is never
# named, and short enough that a pile gets named the week after it goes quiet.
# `richos-rechecks` is the useful demonstration: the largest single directory under
# ~/ab, declared throwaway, and the rule still leaves it alone because it was
# touched yesterday.
SCRATCH_CAMPAIGN_RETENTION_DAYS="7"

# SCRATCH_LEGACY_TMP_PATTERNS — the name families that exist under $TMPDIR
# TODAY, from 171 engine files that call mktemp directly. Swept when older than
# SCRATCH_LEGACY_AGE_HOURS and held open by no process.
#
# THIS DECLARATION IS MEANT TO SHRINK TO NOTHING. It is the migration ramp: as
# callers move to scripts/lib/scratch.sh their names leave this list, and a
# family that no longer appears under $TMPDIR is a family that can be deleted
# from here. A permanent list of names would be the allowlist again.
#
# MEASURED under $TMPDIR 2026-09-18 (87,330 entries, 3.80 GB after the 105 GB
# was removed by hand): hook-staleness.* 685, ep-lint.* 190,
# detect-tx-default.* 73, hook-staleness-engine.* 36, mutation.* 30,
# contract-integrity.* 29, guard-isolation-*.* 28+17+15, mutation-pool.* 27,
# mutation-sandbox.* 15, aborig.* 15, harness-dry.* 11, routing-repro.* 5,
# and thousands of richos-native-*/richos-provision-*/richos-svc* left by
# suites. root-mutation.* is first because it is the one that cost 105 GB.
SCRATCH_LEGACY_TMP_PATTERNS="root-mutation.* mutation.* mutation-pool.* mutation-sandbox.* mutant-sandbox.* harness-dry.* hook-staleness.* hook-staleness-engine.* contract-integrity.* contract-integrity-*.* ep-lint.* detect-tx-default.* guard-isolation-*.* guard-wt-removal.* routing-repro.* aborig.* byref.* ws-spec-* ws-e2e* ws-fourteen* ws-ignored-dir* wtremove* workspace-gate* workspace-lifecycle* live-proof-committed.* demo-canary.* worktree-removal-mutations.* stated-actions-mutations.* richos-svc* richos-skills-fixture* richos-native-* richos-provision-* richos-doctrine-* richos-declination-* richos-steering-* richos-verify-* richos-engine-verify* richos-runtime-*"

# SCRATCH_LEGACY_AGE_HOURS — how old a legacy-family directory must be before
# it is swept. TWO HOURS, and it is deliberately far tighter than the 60-minute
# floor's cousin above is generous.
#
# MEASURED: the 105 GB directory reached that size inside ONE run, so waiting a
# working day to reclaim 100 GB is not a policy, it is the incident again.
#
# TWO HOURS DOES NOT CUT SHORT THE 181-MINUTE MUTATION PASS, and the reason is
# that the age is taken from the NEWEST mtime in the tree, not from its
# creation. A running harness is writing into its sandbox continuously, so its
# newest mtime is always seconds old and it never ages past this floor however
# long it runs. Age here means "nothing has touched this for two hours", which
# is a statement about abandonment rather than about duration. The lsof
# open-handle check sits behind it as the second answer: a process holding the
# directory open makes it INDETERMINATE, never a candidate.
SCRATCH_LEGACY_AGE_HOURS="2"

# SCRATCH_LEGACY_GIT_IS_FIXTURE — whether a `.git` inside a DECLARED legacy
# family under $TMPDIR is treated as harness scaffolding (1) or as a checkout
# that wall 2 refuses to touch (0).
#
# MEASURED, and the measurement is the argument: the first run of the widened
# legacy sweep returned 2,800 INDETERMINATE entries and ALL 2,800 were wall 2.
# Every one was a harness fixture that builds a throwaway repository —
# richos-provision-git / -fresh / -ignore / -valid / -install-home and nine
# more siblings at about 249 each, ws-spec-* at 52, byref.* — 0.61 GB and
# 2,800 directory entries of test scaffolding.
#
# LEAVING THEM UNDECIDABLE IS NOT THE CAUTIOUS CHOICE. Undecidable makes the
# run exit 3, and exit 3 is what the disk watchdog turns into a MASSIVE ALERT.
# An alert that fires on 2,800 test fixtures every six hours gets switched off
# within a day, and then the next 105 GB arrives with nobody watching. A
# permanent unclearable alert is noise, and noise is what makes a real signal
# get ignored.
#
# THE NARROWING IS NARROW. It downgrades wall 2 ONLY, ONLY for names matching
# SCRATCH_LEGACY_TMP_PATTERNS, ONLY for direct children of $TMPDIR, and only
# after the age floor and the machine-wide open-handle check have both passed.
# WALL 3 IS UNTOUCHED — a registered workspace, or anything containing or
# inside one, is still refused, and wall 3 is what was ever protecting a real
# checkout here. The allocator root, the claude scratch roots and the nightly
# all still get wall 2 in full.
#
# Set to 0 to restore the old behavior; the cost is a permanently undecided
# verdict on this machine.
SCRATCH_LEGACY_GIT_IS_FIXTURE="1"

# SCRATCH_SESSION_PROCESS_NAMES — the process names that ARE sessions on this
# machine, space-separated, compared against the basename of `ps -o comm=`.
#
# This is declared rather than hard-coded because the whole safety argument
# rests on it: a scratch directory is deleted only when EVERY session process
# on the machine is accounted for, and a session process this list does not
# name is a session the reaper cannot see. MEASURED 2026-09-17: the running
# session appears as `claude` (pid 15449, `ps -Ao comm=`). The day the vendor
# renames its binary, or somebody runs it under a wrapper, that argument stops
# holding — silently — and this line is the one place to repair it. A vendor's
# name for its own process is exactly the kind of third-party fact that is
# never adopted by assumption.
SCRATCH_SESSION_PROCESS_NAMES="claude"

# SCRATCH_AGE_FLOOR_MINUTES — how old a dead thing must be before it is
# deleted. This is NOT the liveness test (liveness is decided from the process
# table and the session registry, never from an mtime); it is the margin
# against the race where a session has started and has not yet recorded
# itself.
#
# MEASURED: the live session's process started at "Wed Sep 16 23:31:27 2026"
# and its registration was written at 2026-09-16T23:31:28Z — ONE SECOND. 60
# minutes is that latency multiplied by 3,600 and still sweeps the same
# working day, so the floor costs nothing and covers a registration three
# orders of magnitude slower than the one that was measured.
SCRATCH_AGE_FLOOR_MINUTES="60"

# SCRATCH_NIGHTLY_DIR / SCRATCH_NIGHTLY_KEEP — the nightly's per-run build
# products. Only `releases/` and `logs/` are ever considered, newest N kept.
#
# NEVER `source/`, and that is a finding rather than a precaution:
# ~/.richos-nightly/source IS A REGISTERED GIT WORKTREE of the richos
# repository (`git worktree add --detach`, richos/app/scripts/nightly-local.py
# line 184), and at 5.89 GB of that directory's 6.2 GB it is ALSO the largest
# single pile on the machine — 5.74 GB of it the build cache under
# source/richos/app. A reaper deleting inside a registered worktree is the
# failure this engine retired com.richos.worktree-reconciler for, so the cache
# stays here and its right owner is the nightly itself. NEVER `runtime/`
# either: 322 MB of downloaded toolchains (node 199 MB, python 66 MB, git
# 43 MB) that cost a network fetch to replace rather than a rebuild.
#
# KEEP=3 measured 2026-09-17: one release is 146 MB, one run's log is ~140 KB,
# the nightly publishes every release to GitHub as well, and three is the
# smallest number that still lets a person compare a bad build against the two
# before it. 3 x 146 MB = 438 MB standing cost.
SCRATCH_NIGHTLY_DIR="$HOME/.richos-nightly"
SCRATCH_NIGHTLY_KEEP="3"

# SCRATCH_NOTICE_BYTES — the SessionStart notice says nothing at all below this
# much reclaimable scratch. 1 GiB, measured: single sessions' scratchpads on
# this machine ran 336 MB / 658 MB / 4,270 MB, so one GiB is "more than an
# ordinary session's worth is lying around dead" and a quiet machine stays
# quiet. A banner that speaks every time is a banner that stops being read.
SCRATCH_NOTICE_BYTES="1073741824"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE GARBAGE ALARM — added 2026-09-18, and it is the half of §54 the mechanism
# did not have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frank-opus-garbage1, on the state of the alerting before this:
#   "Its alarm is a disk-space alarm and a delete-failure alarm. It has no
#    garbage alarm. So garbage that no arm looks at produces neither a cleanup
#    nor an alert."
# The CEO's sentence has two halves — always cleaned up, OR Rich gets a MASSIVE
# ALERT — and they were satisfied simultaneously only for the paths already
# swept. Everything else fell between them silently.
#
# SKIPPED is the new number, and it is deliberately not `kept`. A kept entry is
# alive or young and will be collected in due course. A SKIPPED entry is one this
# program has LOOKED AT and will never take: another program's temp directory,
# another user's, a symlink, or an entry a budgeted run did not reach. Nobody is
# coming for it unless a person removes it.

# SCRATCH_SKIPPED_NOTICE_BYTES — below this, the skipped pile is silent.
#
# 1 GiB, and it is measured to SPEAK TODAY rather than to be comfortable. On this
# machine on 2026-09-18 the skipped pile is 1.13 GB in 2,934 places, of which
# 942 MB is a single directory — com.microsoft.VSCode.ShipIt.V5TnjgtN, a VS Code
# installer's leftovers that nothing on this machine will ever collect. That is
# exactly the thing §54 says Rich should be told about and then delete by hand,
# and once he has, the pile drops to ~0.18 GB and the line goes quiet on its own.
# A threshold chosen so that today's real pile is below it would be a threshold
# chosen to avoid saying anything.
SCRATCH_SKIPPED_NOTICE_BYTES="1073741824"

# SCRATCH_UNDECIDABLE_NOTICE_BYTES — below this, the undecidable pile is silent.
#
# 64 MiB, AND A SEPARATE NUMBER FROM SCRATCH_NOTICE_BYTES ON PURPOSE. They shared
# one threshold, which is Frank's D12: up to a GiB of permanently undecidable
# garbage was invisible on every channel, because a 1 GiB bar written for "an
# ordinary session's worth of dead scratch" is far too high for a pile that NO
# SCHEDULED RUN WILL EVER CLEAR. The two are different in kind — one shrinks by
# itself, the other never does — so they get different numbers.
#
# MEASURED: today's undecidable pile is 164,580,800 B in 23 places, and every one
# is wall 3 refusing a `richos-owned-wake-native-*/workspace` that is still in the
# worktree ledger pointing at a nine-day-old temp fixture. That is a real, fixable
# second-order problem (stale ledger rows), it is exactly what somebody should be
# told about, and 64 MiB is low enough to say so while staying quiet about the
# odd transient lsof failure.
SCRATCH_UNDECIDABLE_NOTICE_BYTES="67108864"

# SCRATCH_FAILURES_STATE / SCRATCH_REAPER_STATE — the two files the sweeper
# publishes and the watchdog reads. DECLARED, because they were not, and that was
# Frank's D9.
#
# THE DEFECT, IN ONE SENTENCE: the sweeper's failures_path() honored
# CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR and the watchdog's reader did not, and neither path was
# declared anywhere, so nothing reconciled them. One exported variable in an
# operator's shell profile — and the engine's own suites export it — sent the
# sweeper's failure record to one directory and the alert's reader to another.
# Frank proved it: the reaper wrote the failure under $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/state/,
# `disk-watchdog.sh --alert` exited 0 and printed nothing, and the MASSIVE ALERT
# about a garbage path that could not be deleted went silently nowhere.
#
# Both programs now resolve the base the same way AND read the same declaration,
# so it takes two independent failures to separate them again.
SCRATCH_FAILURES_STATE="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/state/scratch-failures.json"
SCRATCH_REAPER_STATE="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/state/scratch-reaper-state.json"

# APP_INSTANCE_FAILURES_STATE — the same treatment for the test-instance record
# (§54 addendum 4). It was read with a hard-coded ~/.claude default at both ends
# and declared nowhere; that is the D9 shape waiting to happen again.
APP_INSTANCE_FAILURES_STATE="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/state/app-instance-failures.json"

# TEST_DEVICE_FAILURES_STATE — test simulators and emulators that could not be
# removed, or whose owner cannot be proven while they run (§54; 2026-09-22,
# three simulators booted by killed test runs outlived the session). Written by
# scripts/lib/testdevices.py, which resolves the same default; read by the disk
# watchdog for the MASSIVE ALERT. Rows resolve when the device is gone.
TEST_DEVICE_FAILURES_STATE="${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/state/test-device-failures.json"

# SCRATCH_REAPER_HOURS / SCRATCH_REAPER_MINUTE — when the launchd job fires
# (local time, StartCalendarInterval). Every six hours.
#
# 04:40 is the load-bearing one: the nightly ran 22:57-03:53 on the night this
# was written, so 04:40 is after it and before anybody is working. :40 is
# offset from com.richos.ci-surface-watch's :20 so the two scheduled jobs never
# contend for the same minute. Six-hourly rather than daily because the pile
# that triggered this grew 19 GB inside ONE working day.
SCRATCH_REAPER_HOURS="4 10 16 22"
SCRATCH_REAPER_MINUTE="40"

# SCRATCH_DOCKER_PRUNE / SCRATCH_DOCKER_KEEP_STORAGE — the Docker arm of the
# scheduled sweep. CEO, 2026-09-18 (§54 addendum 3), on being shown that
# Docker's caches grow with nothing removing them.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS MACHINE 2026-09-18, `docker system df`:
#   Images       38 total, 15 active, 31.7 GB, 19.46 GB reclaimable (61%)
#   Build Cache  209 entries, 16.71 GB, 8.509 GB reclaimable
# Docker's data root is /Volumes/E1TB/vm/docker/DockerDesktop (33 GB) — the # machine-path-exempt: retained historical measurement or design record; never a storage default
# EXTERNAL SSD, not Macintosh HD, so none of this is on the volume the
# watchdog's primary threshold guards. It is swept because unbounded growth on
# any volume is the thing the rule forbids, not because it is urgent.
#
# THE STANDING RULE, IN THE CEO'S WORDS (2026-09-18): "Go for stricter standing
# rule i.e. automatically dropping unused named images after 30 days" — chosen
# after being told the caveat that STOPPED CONTAINERS PIN IMAGES.
#
# How that caveat shows up in the implementation: the three stages run in this
# order and the order is load-bearing.
#
#   1. docker container prune -f --filter until=720h
#        Stopped containers older than 30 days. RUNNING CONTAINERS ARE NEVER
#        CANDIDATES AT ANY AGE — `container prune` only ever considers stopped
#        ones, which is why no extra guard is needed to protect a running one.
#        This runs FIRST because an exited container pins its image: the other
#        way round, every image held by a months-old dead container survives
#        stage 2 for a reason nothing in the log would explain.
#   2. docker image prune -a -f --filter until=720h
#        Every image no container references that is older than 30 days, NAMED
#        OR NOT. This is the stricter rule, and it is what finally reclaims the
#        ~17.8 GB of unused-but-TAGGED images measured on 2026-09-18 that a
#        dangling-only prune cannot touch (4 dangling images, ~1.7 GB, was the
#        whole of what dangling-only would have returned).
#   3. docker builder prune -f --keep-storage 20GB
#
# WHY -a IS SAFE HERE WHEN IT WOULD NOT BE ALONE: the age filter. An image
# pulled or built this month is never a candidate however unreferenced it is
# today, so the rule cannot delete the image somebody is about to use — only
# one nobody has used for a month.
#
# 720h = 30 days, expressed in the unit Docker's --filter actually accepts.
#
# 20GB matches the defaultKeepStorage already declared in ~/.docker/daemon.json
# — deliberately the same number, so the scheduled prune and the daemon's own
# GC cannot disagree about the floor and thrash against each other.
#
# A STOPPED DAEMON IS SKIPPED SILENTLY AND IS NEVER A FAILURE. Docker Desktop
# is shut down most of the time on a laptop, and a scheduled job that logged a
# failure every six hours for an optional tool would train its reader to skip
# the log entirely.
#   4. the anonymous unused VOLUMES, added 2026-09-18 (Frank's D14)
#        MEASURED: 21 dangling volumes, 402.2 MB, and no arm of the sweep had an
#        opinion about any of them. The ruling named containers, images and build
#        cache, so this is a gap against "always cleaned up" rather than against
#        his words — and unbounded growth on any volume is what the rule forbids.
#
#        THE BRIEF ASKED FOR `docker volume prune --filter until=...` AND THAT
#        FILTER DOES NOT EXIST. Checked before it was built:
#          $ docker volume prune --help
#            --filter filter   Provide filter values (e.g. "label=<label>")
#          $ docker version --format '{{.Server.Version}}'  ->  29.2.0
#        and the vendor's reference says "The currently supported filters are:
#        label". Container, image and builder prune all take `until`; volume prune
#        does not. Copying the prescription would have shipped either a command
#        that errors every run or, worse, one whose unknown filter is ignored and
#        which then prunes with NO AGE LIMIT AT ALL.
#
#        So the age is applied by us, from `docker volume inspect --format
#        '{{.CreatedAt}}'`, and only to ANONYMOUS volumes — which is Docker's own
#        default rather than a choice of ours, since `volume prune` needs `-a`
#        before it will touch a named one. A name is somebody having meant it.
SCRATCH_DOCKER_PRUNE="1"
SCRATCH_DOCKER_KEEP_STORAGE="20GB"
SCRATCH_DOCKER_UNTIL="720h"

# SCRATCH_DOCKER_THROWAWAY_IMAGES / SCRATCH_DOCKER_CONTAINER_ALERT_DAYS —
# A RUNNING CONTAINER IS IMMORTAL BY DESIGN, SO IT GETS REPORTED (Frank's D14).
#
# `container prune` only ever considers STOPPED containers, and the code says so as
# a virtue: "a running one is not a candidate at any age". That is right as a
# DELETION rule and it leaves a hole. Measured 2026-09-18:
#
#   $ docker ps --format '{{.Names}} | {{.Image}} | up {{.RunningFor}} | {{.Command}}'
#   rl55 | richos-linux:git-latest | up 8 days ago | "sleep infinity"
#   rlx  | richos-linux:24.04      | up 8 days ago | "sleep infinity"
#
# Two `sleep infinity` dev shells, eight days old, pinning 514 MB and 349 MB of
# image so `image prune -a` can never reclaim them. Nothing alerted, and that is
# how dev containers are actually used, so it recurs.
#
# NOTHING IS EVER STOPPED AUTOMATICALLY. Four of the six containers running on this
# machine are the Buzz production stack, and stopping a running container is a
# category of action this program does not take. A match is REPORTED as a standing
# entry, which reaches the garbage alarm, and a person ends it — the same shape as
# a campaign root, for the same reason.
#
# The image list is EMPTY-BY-DEFAULT and narrow: nothing is a throwaway unless
# somebody declares it. `richos-linux:*` and `richos-ci*` are ours and are built to
# be discarded; a postgres or redis image never appears here.
SCRATCH_DOCKER_THROWAWAY_IMAGES="richos-linux:* richos-ci:* richos-linuxci:*"

# 7 days. Measured against the pair above at 8 days, and against the honest use:
# a dev shell wanted for an afternoon is normal, one still up a week later has been
# forgotten. Shorter would fire on a container somebody is actively living in.
SCRATCH_DOCKER_CONTAINER_ALERT_DAYS="7"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DENY-BY-DEFAULT TEMP ARM — added 2026-09-18 after the mechanism was
# attacked and DEFEATED BY SCOPE rather than by cleverness.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# frank-opus-garbage1's verdict, in his words:
#   "The mechanism is not defeatable in the sense of being tricked — it is
#    defeated by NOT BEING ASKED. Its alarm is a disk-space alarm and a
#    delete-failure alarm. It has no garbage alarm."
#
# MEASURED AGAIN HERE BEFORE ANY OF IT WAS BELIEVED (2026-09-18, this machine):
#   direct children of $TMPDIR                   60,942
#   covered by a declared pattern                 4,172   (0.08 GB)
#   INVISIBLE to every arm                       56,770   (1.95 GB)
#   direct children of /private/tmp                 764, of which 97 are root's
#   swept by any arm before today                     1   (claude-501)
#
# AND THE ARGUMENT THAT SETTLES THE SHAPE, which is not the byte count. The
# biggest invisible family on this disk is `richos-owned-wake-native-*` — 42
# entries, 198 MB, nine days old — and its creator NO LONGER EXISTS IN THE TREE:
#   $ grep -rln 'owned-wake-native' /Users/alex/ab/richos/richos   -> no matches
# An allowlist of names can only ever contain names somebody read off a creator.
# A creator that has been deleted cannot be read, so the enumeration cannot
# converge, and every future family arrives invisible exactly as this one did.
# A KEEP-LIST OF FOREIGN OWNERS RUNS THE OTHER WAY: it names other people's
# software, it is short, and it stops growing.

# SCRATCH_TMP_DENY_BY_DEFAULT — 1 inverts $TMPDIR and the shared temp roots from
# an allowlist of OUR names to a keep-list of FOREIGN ones. 0 restores the
# pre-2026-09-18 behavior, at the cost of 56,770 invisible entries.
SCRATCH_TMP_DENY_BY_DEFAULT="1"

# SCRATCH_SHARED_TMP_ROOTS — temp roots that are NOT $TMPDIR and that nothing
# reaches through it, swept by the same deny-by-default rule.
#
# /private/tmp, and Frank measured 25.77 GiB there that no arm had an opinion
# about — including a single 19 GiB directory of two-day-old evidence named
# `richos-*`, on the volume the watchdog guards, which nothing would ever have
# removed. The two claude roots live inside it and are swept by their own arm;
# this arm skips them by name so nothing is ever decided twice.
SCRATCH_SHARED_TMP_ROOTS="/private/tmp"

# SCRATCH_BULK_TMP_ROOTS — the producers' own temp roots on the bulk drive,
# RELATIVE TO RICHOS_BULK_ROOT (machine settings), swept like a shared temp root:
# deny-by-default for their direct children, and owner-based for the allocator
# root inside each. tmp/richos-gates is the nightly gates' TMPDIR
# (nightly-local.py gate_tmpdir), tmp/codex is Codex's TMPDIR, and
# tmp/autocheck-tests is autocheck.test.py's fixture parent. Measured 2026-10-23:
# 7.8 GB, 5.1 GB and 116 MB of test fixtures there, none of it ever looked at,
# because the scheduled job's TMPDIR is launchd's. With no bulk root configured
# nothing is added; with one configured and unmounted the sweep refuses to run.
SCRATCH_BULK_TMP_ROOTS="tmp/richos-gates tmp/codex tmp/autocheck-tests"

# SCRATCH_FIXTURE_REPO_LABELS — the allocator labels (scratch.sh `scratch_new
# <label>`) of suites that make ONLY fixture repositories in their allocation.
# A repository inside an ENDED allocation is deleted only when its ledger row
# carries one of these labels (cleanup PRD R1: positive evidence, never a name or
# a row alone). Measured 2026-10-23: these three suites clean up on pass, fail and
# TERM, and SIGKILL left their fixture repositories forever (e.g.
# 26260-operator-fences-suite-*, 940K, two .git directories). Anything else with
# a repository in it stays undecided and is reported.
SCRATCH_FIXTURE_REPO_LABELS="operator-fences-suite quota-watch-test resource-waits-test"

# SCRATCH_CODEX_HOME / SCRATCH_CODEX_WORKTREE_ROOTS — Codex's own finished
# worktrees (cleanup PRD section 3 and A6; CEO decision §47 authorizes deleting a
# plain codex/ worktree whose commits are all in main). The workspace engine never
# touches codex/ (its point 2), so nothing collected them: measured 2026-10-23,
# about 67 GB in 104 checkouts. SCRATCH_CODEX_HOME is the app's home (its
# attachment records, read-only, and its worktree root). The roots are absolute,
# or relative to RICHOS_BULK_ROOT. Only these roots are ever looked in; a
# repository's other worktrees are never candidates.
SCRATCH_CODEX_HOME="$HOME/.codex"
SCRATCH_CODEX_WORKTREE_ROOTS="$HOME/.codex/worktrees ab/worktrees"

# SCRATCH_FOREIGN_PATTERNS — the keep-list. Other people's software, never ours.
# Matched against the NAME of a direct child of a temp root; a match is KEPT,
# counted, and reported, so a foreign pile is visible without being touched.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ENTRY, and only the first kind is meant to be permanent.
#
#   1. THE STRUCTURAL RULE: a reverse-DNS name. macOS gives every application a
#      per-bundle-id directory under the user temp root, and every one of them
#      belongs to the program named in it. This is a rule about the SHAPE of the
#      name, so it covers software that is not installed yet.
#      `com.richos.*` IS DELIBERATELY INCLUDED: that directory is created by the
#      operating system for the installed app, not allocated by our code, and the
#      operator's own running app is the last thing this program should touch.
#   2. NAMED FAMILIES measured on this disk on 2026-09-18, with their counts:
#        com.*                131   (incl. com.microsoft.VSCode.ShipIt, 942 MB)
#        .com.*             1,964   (Codex CLI)
#        org.* net.*             2   (WebKit Playwright, WhatsApp)
#        BlobRegistryFiles*   532   (WebKit)
#        TemporaryDirectory.* 173   (Foundation / SwiftPM)
#        _Users_* _private_*   22   (SwiftPM path-slug build locks)
#        TemporaryItems         1   node-compile-cache 1  (Node.js)
#        xcrun_*                1   .AddressBookLocks 1   vscode-* 2
#        codex-*                1 in $TMPDIR, 2 in /private/tmp
#        claude-*               2 in /private/tmp (incl. the MCP browser bridge's
#                                   live socket directory)
#        powerlog 1, boost_interprocess 1 in /private/tmp
#      Total matched: 2,831 of 60,943 in $TMPDIR, 8 of 764 in /private/tmp.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE AND DOES NOT NEED TO BE: the 96 root-owned `tmp-mount-*`
# directories macOS leaves in /private/tmp. The arm refuses anything not owned by
# the running uid, which is a structural wall rather than a name, and it is why
# this list does not have to grow every time another daemon uses /private/tmp.
SCRATCH_FOREIGN_PATTERNS="com.* .com.* org.* .org.* net.* .net.* io.* .io.* dev.* .dev.* app.* .app.* TemporaryItems TemporaryDirectory.* _Users_* _private_* _Volumes_* node-compile-cache BlobRegistryFiles* xcrun_* .AddressBookLocks powerlog boost_interprocess vscode-* claude-* codex-* .codex*"

# SCRATCH_UNKNOWN_AGE_HOURS — the age floor for a name no arm declares.
#
# THE AGE IS THE SECOND CONDITION, NEVER THE PROOF. The proof is the one
# scan_orphan already uses: the newest mtime in the tree PREDATES THE START OF
# EVERY RUNNING SESSION PROCESS, so no running session can have written it and
# none can write it again. Measured on this machine 2026-09-18: one running
# session, started "Wed Sep 16 23:31:27 2026" under TZ=UTC0, so the proof alone
# already refuses everything touched in the last day and a half.
#
# 72 HOURS, AND THE THREE DAYS ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN ROUND. Every pile Frank
# named is older than it — richos-owned-wake-native 9.1 d, richos-setup 9.7 d,
# richos-corpus 10.4 d, richos-ecs-assessment 5.0 d, the census-* trees 4.0 d —
# so three days collects all of it. And the thing three days buys over one is the
# case that matters: a teammate's evidence directory from yesterday afternoon
# survives even if every session on the machine restarts overnight, which is the
# one way the session-start proof alone can be beaten. Deleting an agent's
# uncommitted evidence is the "data loss, not garbage" failure, and it is worth
# two extra days of a few hundred megabytes to make it impossible.
SCRATCH_UNKNOWN_AGE_HOURS="72"

# SCRATCH_UNKNOWN_GIT_IS_FIXTURE — whether a `.git` under an UNDECLARED temp name
# is treated as harness scaffolding (1) or as a checkout wall 2 refuses (0).
#
# THIS WAS WRITTEN AS 0 AND THE MEASUREMENT CHANGED IT. The reasoning for 0 was
# that an undeclared name might hold somebody's real work, so a handful of
# INDETERMINATE entries is a thing a person should read. Then the arm was run:
#
#   $ scratch-reaper.sh --json     (deny-by-default on, this key at 0)
#   TOTAL INDETERMINATE   327 entries   288,360,636 bytes
#   ... and ALL 327 are wall 2, across 177 families:
#       other-<pid>-wt   93   (git-worktree fixtures)
#       richos-owned-wake-native  49        census-git / census-gitnr  4
#       detect-nonnative-worktree  1        guard-sealed-test  1
#       engine-status 1, reconcile-test 2, sage-ro 2, qrepro 2, ...
#
# NOT ONE IS A CHECKOUT. Every one is a harness that builds a throwaway
# repository under a name it invented, and the largest family's creator does not
# exist in the tree any more, so nobody can go and fix it either. 327 permanent
# undecidable entries would make the run exit 3 for ever, and exit 3 is what the
# watchdog turns into a MASSIVE ALERT — the unclearable-noise failure that killed
# the 2,800-fixture case for SCRATCH_LEGACY_GIT_IS_FIXTURE above.
#
# AND THE PRECONDITIONS HERE ARE STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE LEGACY ARM'S, which
# is what makes 1 defensible rather than merely convenient. Before wall 2 is even
# consulted this arm has established: nothing running can own it (no session
# process on the machine existed when it was last written), 72 h untouched rather
# than 2 h, owned by this uid, and no process holds a file open inside it. WALL 3
# IS UNTOUCHED — a registered workspace, or anything containing or inside one, is
# still refused, and wall 3 is the wall that was ever protecting a real checkout.
# Nobody clones their work into $TMPDIR/other-11090-wt and leaves it untouched
# for three days with no open handle while every session on the machine restarts.
#
# Set to 0 to restore wall 2 in full; the cost is a permanently undecided verdict
# on this machine and an alert nobody can clear.
SCRATCH_UNKNOWN_GIT_IS_FIXTURE="1"

# SCRATCH_APP_REGISTRY_RELPATH — where an instance of the app keeps its list of
# connected repositories, relative to whatever HOME it was launched with.
#
# THE ONE THING INSIDE A FINISHED SESSION'S SCRATCHPAD THAT IS NOT GARBAGE. A QA
# fixture repository is built inside a session scratchpad and its absolute path
# is written into this file; an app instance that is STILL RUNNING is then
# pointed at a directory the reaper is entitled to remove. Removing it is not
# reclaiming garbage, it is pulling a repository out from under a running
# program. Every root named by a registry belonging to a LIVE instance is kept,
# and dead instances' registries protect nothing.
#
# A RELATIVE PATH AND NOT AN ABSOLUTE ONE, because a test instance is launched
# with HOME inside scratch: the operator's own registry is found under $HOME,
# and each test instance's is found in its own open files (macOS will not show
# another process's environment, but lsof shows what it has open — the argument
# scripts/lib/appinstances.py is built on).
SCRATCH_APP_REGISTRY_RELPATH="Library/Application Support/com.richos.app/entities.json"

# SCRATCH_SYSTEM_FLAGS — the file-flag mask that means THE OPERATING SYSTEM OWNS
# THIS, so the sweeper classifies it once and never attempts or reports it again.
#
# CEO, 2026-09-19, reading the status line: "And what is this all about: MASSIVE
# ALERT — DISK: 218 path(s) could not be deleted (delete BY HAND)". Eighteen of
# those 218 were directories macOS creates under the per-user temp root for its
# own agents — AudioComponentRegistrar, SandboxHelper, talagent, studentd,
# StatusKitAgent, ${DaemonNameOrIdentifierHere} and twelve more. Each is 64 bytes
# and empty, and each had failed with `Operation not permitted` on all 52
# attempts since 2026-09-18T12:15:05Z. §54's "the clean-up failed" branch asks a
# PERSON to go and delete something; there was nothing here for a person to do,
# and the alert had been asking for a day.
#
# 0x00160000 = SF_NOUNLINK|SF_IMMUTABLE|SF_APPEND. Measured on this machine,
# 18 of 18 carry SF_NOUNLINK (and the `com.apple.rootless` extended attribute):
#
#   $ ls -ldO $TMPDIR/talagent
#   drwx------@ 2 alex staff sunlnk 64 7 Sep 19:57 .../talagent
#
# A FLAG AND NOT A LIST OF NAMES. macOS adds agents with every point release and
# none of these names exists anywhere in this tree, so a name list converges on
# the day somebody stops maintaining it — the same argument SCRATCH_FOREIGN_PATTERNS
# makes about allowlists. A flag the kernel sets covers next year's daemon.
#
# UF_IMMUTABLE (`uchg`, 0x2) IS NOT IN THIS MASK ON PURPOSE. The sweeper's retry
# clears exactly that flag and then succeeds: an obstacle we can remove is not an
# owner. Only the SF_* flags, which need root to set or clear, mean "not ours".
SCRATCH_SYSTEM_FLAGS="0x00160000"

# SCRATCH_REAPER_STATE_MAX_AGE_HOURS — how old the sweeper's published numbers
# may be before the watchdog says so instead of quoting them as fact.
#
# CEO, 2026-09-19, reading the status line: "11.4 GB in 2 place(s) UNDECIDABLE —
# no run will clear it". He had deleted both of those piles by hand an hour
# earlier and the live sweep said undecidable=1 at 2,176 bytes. The watchdog was
# quoting a state file written by the 12:10Z scheduled pass, with nothing on the
# line to say it was six hours old.
#
# 12 HOURS = TWO CADENCES. The scheduled pass runs every six (SCRATCH_REAPER_HOURS
# above), so one missed run is tolerated and two is a number that is no longer
# evidence. Inside the window the figure is printed with the time it was measured;
# outside it, the reader is told the measurement is stale and given the command
# that re-derives it. `--status` never quotes either way: it runs the sweeper.
SCRATCH_REAPER_STATE_MAX_AGE_HOURS="12"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_* — a running test instance of the app is garbage too
#                      (scripts/lib/appinstances.py)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CEO, §54 addendum 4, verbatim: "Ray had left the test app window open. Test
# app windows must always close/quit when testing is finished. Same hygiene as
# with any other garbage." The candidate-.7 walk ended with the QA instance
# still on his screen after the handoff.
#
# THE PATH SET IS NOT DECLARED HERE, DELIBERATELY. appinstances.py derives it
# from the SCRATCH_* keys above — the session scratchpads, the allocator root,
# and both $TMPDIR pattern families — so a name family added to the reaper's
# coverage is covered for processes the same day, with no second edit and no
# second definition to drift. The keys below are only the things the reaper has
# no opinion about: which executables are the app, and how long to wait.

# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_PROCESS_NAMES — the executables that ARE the app.
#
# MEASURED, not assumed: richos/app/src-tauri/Cargo.toml declares the package
# `richos-tauri`, and the BUNDLED app's executable carries the same name
# (RichOS.app/Contents/MacOS/richos-tauri) even though tauri.conf.json sets
# productName=RichOS. So this name matches the CEO's installed app as well as a
# test build, and it is only a SHORTLIST — a process named here is still never
# quit unless it is positively placed under a scratch root. A false entry here
# costs one lsof; it can never cost a wrongly-quit window.
APP_TEST_INSTANCE_PROCESS_NAMES="richos-tauri"

# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_BUNDLE_ID — the identifier whose per-user state directories
# mean "this is the operator's own instance". From src-tauri/tauri.conf.json.
#
# It is used ONLY to build the never-collect set (~/Library/Application
# Support/<id> and siblings). It is NEVER used to address a quit: an osascript
# `tell application id "<id>" to quit` reaches whichever instance the window
# server registered for it, which on this machine is the CEO's. Quits are
# addressed by pid through System Events.
APP_TEST_INSTANCE_BUNDLE_ID="com.richos.app"

# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_REAL_HOME_PATHS — extra absolute paths that mean "the
# operator's own instance", space-separated, `~` and `%u` expanded. Empty by
# default because the bundle-id directories above plus ~/.richos*, ~/RichOS
# already cover this machine.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT "ANYTHING UNDER $HOME": a legitimate test instance's EXECUTABLE
# normally lives under $HOME (a git worktree's target/, or ~/Applications/
# RichOS.app launched against a scratch HOME), so a blanket $HOME test would
# classify every instance as the operator's and collect nothing at all. What
# distinguishes whose instance it is, is where its STATE lives.
#
# Measured on this machine 2026-09-18, and worth recording because the brief
# that ordered this work named two paths that are not here:
#   /Applications/RichOS.app   — does not exist
#   ~/myrichos-nightly-*       — does not exist
#   ~/Applications/RichOS.app  — EXISTS, and is the installed app
#   ~/.richos-nightly          — EXISTS (already on the reaper's never-touch)
# Neither absence matters, because nothing is protected by being listed: it is
# protected by not being under a scratch root.
APP_TEST_INSTANCE_REAL_HOME_PATHS=""

# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_QUIT_GRACE_SECONDS — how long a pid-targeted quit request is
# given before SIGTERM. 8s: a Tauri app on this machine takes 1-3s to tear down
# its WebKit processes and flush state, and the whole point of asking first is
# that it gets to save. Below ~5s the graceful path would almost never win and
# the mechanism would be a kill wearing a politer name.
APP_TEST_INSTANCE_QUIT_GRACE_SECONDS="8"

# APP_TEST_INSTANCE_KILL_GRACE_SECONDS — the wait after SIGTERM before SIGKILL,
# and again after SIGKILL before declaring a survivor. 3s, the same value
# mega-lander/workspaces.py has used for RICHOS_WORKSPACES_STOP_GRACE since the
# workspace spec landed; an instance that has ignored both a quit request and a
# TERM is not going to answer on the fourth second.
APP_TEST_INSTANCE_KILL_GRACE_SECONDS="3"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISK_* — the disk watchdog (scripts/disk-watchdog.sh)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CEO, 2026-09-18 (ceo-decisions §54 addendum 1), verbatim: "Yeah, a separate
# disk watchdog is needed because the CEO needs to be alerted in case the disk
# storage gets close to running out for any reason."
#
# A SEPARATE MECHANISM, AND THE SEPARATION IS THE REQUIREMENT. It has its own
# launchd timer, and it does not call the sweeper, read the sweeper's
# declarations, or depend on the sweeper having run. The reason is in his words:
# "for any reason". A watchdog wired into the cleanup mechanism can only ever
# report the free space that mechanism knows about — and the disk can fill from
# a video export, a Time Machine local snapshot, an Xcode simulator runtime or
# anything else that has nothing to do with RichOS.
#
# ...AND ADDENDUM 2 IS WHY IT SHOULD NEVER FIRE: "But ideally, the disk watchdog
# will NEVER bark because the RichOS app failed to clean up its garbage. The
# RichOS app must always clean up its garbage automatically, no matter what." So
# every alert carries a CLASSIFICATION — "RichOS garbage (defect)" or "other" —
# because a bark caused by our own garbage is a defect report, not a disk
# warning, and the two want completely different responses.

# DISK_WATCHDOG_ENABLE — 1 (or absent) arms it. Absent means ON.
DISK_WATCHDOG_ENABLE="1"

# DISK_PRIMARY_VOLUME — the volume whose absolute thresholds below are stated in
# gigabytes. Macintosh HD's data volume: the one the OS, the apps and every
# repository live on, and the only one whose exhaustion stops the machine.
DISK_PRIMARY_VOLUME="/System/Volumes/Data"

# Storage volumes resolved by machine settings are also watched. Thresholds scale by
# capacity (see DISK_*_GB below). CEO addendum 3: "The watchdog reports the
# SSD's free space too (a second volume line), same thresholds scaled to its
# size, since Docker, cargo targets and Playwright caches now live there."
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-18: /Volumes/E1TB is 954 GiB with 853 GiB free (11% used), # machine-path-exempt: retained historical measurement or design record; never a storage default
# and it holds Docker's data root at /Volumes/E1TB/vm/docker/DockerDesktop # machine-path-exempt: retained historical measurement or design record; never a storage default
# (33 GB). A volume that is not mounted is SKIPPED silently — an external disk
# being unplugged is not a disk-space event.

# ---- the two audiences, and they have different numbers on purpose ----------
#
# RICH (this session, every session start and every turn end) gets the earliest
# warning, because Rich can act on it without interrupting anybody.
#
# DISK_RICH_ALERT_GB — free space below this is a MASSIVE ALERT to Rich.
# 60 GB. MEASURED: the 105 GB incident took the Data volume from about 170 GB
# free to 49 GB inside a few hours. 60 GB is above the floor that morning
# reached, so the alert would have fired while there was still room to work, and
# it is far enough below the 160-192 GB this machine normally runs at that a
# healthy day is silent.
DISK_RICH_ALERT_GB="60"

# DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB — RETAINED, AND NO LONGER THE TEST. See
# DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB_PER_HOUR below, which replaced it on 2026-09-18. It stays
# declared because the JSON still reports it and a reader comparing the old
# behavior with the new one should be able to see both numbers.
DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB="20"

# DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB_PER_HOUR — a RATE of fall that is a MASSIVE ALERT whatever
# the absolute level. 80 GB/hour.
#
# =========================================================================
# THE RATIONALE THIS REPLACES WAS NOT SUPPORTED BY THE RECORD IT CITED (D8)
# =========================================================================
# It read: "THIS IS THE THRESHOLD THAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT 2026-09-17, and the
# absolute one would not have in time." frank-opus-garbage1 checked it against the
# record and it does not hold. The sandbox was created at 22:39 and the reaper's
# next run was 03:40, so 105 GB arrived across a window of AT LEAST five hours —
# about 21 GB/hour, or roughly 5 GB per 15-minute tick, a QUARTER of the old
# 20 GB-per-tick threshold. If the four copies instead ran back-to-back at local
# disk speed the rate would be far above it, and NEITHER READING IS MEASURED
# ANYWHERE, so the claim was unproven in both directions.
#
# WHAT IS ESTABLISHED: the absolute 60 GB floor WOULD have fired, because free
# space reached 49 GB. So the corrected statement is the one this declaration now
# carries — the absolute floor is what catches an incident like 2026-09-17, and
# THE RATE ALARM'S JOB IS THE FASTER INCIDENT NOBODY HAS MEASURED YET. A rationale
# that said otherwise would be trusted by the next person who tunes this.
#
# 80 GB/HOUR IS EXACTLY THE OLD PER-TICK SENSITIVITY, DELIBERATELY. 20 GB across a
# 15-minute interval IS 80 GB/hour, so nothing about a normal day changes and no
# new false positive is introduced. Lowering it to catch the measured 21 GB/hour
# lower bound of the 2026-09-17 window would mean alerting at 5 GB per tick, and a
# cargo build, a Docker pull or an Xcode archive reaches that legitimately — which
# is the cries-wolf failure that ends with the alarm switched off and the absolute
# floor left doing the work alone anyway.
DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB_PER_HOUR="80"

# DISK_DROP_MAX_GAP_INTERVALS — across a gap longer than this many intervals, NO
# RATE IS COMPUTED AT ALL and the gap is reported instead. 3, i.e. 45 minutes.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (D7): the drop was a subtraction against whatever the
# previous reading happened to be, with no elapsed-time term. Frank aged the
# baseline ten hours — a closed laptop, a wake from sleep, an upgrade window, a
# launchd job unloaded and reloaded — and the watchdog printed
# "DROPPED 30.0 GB since the last reading ... CLASSIFICATION: RichOS garbage
# (DEFECT)". 3 GB/hour is a normal working day on this machine, and it was
# reported as our defect.
#
# WHY REFUSING IS BETTER THAN DIVIDING. A fall averaged over ten hours says
# nothing about what happened in any one of them, so a rate derived from it is not
# a weaker measurement, it is a different quantity wearing the same name. Three
# intervals is generous enough that a slow tick or a briefly busy machine does not
# trip it, and short enough that a laptop that was asleep is never averaged over.
DISK_DROP_MAX_GAP_INTERVALS="3"

# DISK_DROP_MIN_GAP_SECONDS — below this gap between readings, NO RATE IS COMPUTED
# either. 60 seconds.
#
# THIS IS A HOLE THE D7 FIX OPENED, NOT ONE IT INHERITED, and it was caught by the
# suite rather than reasoned about: turning a difference into a rate makes the
# DENOMINATOR dangerous at both ends, and a sub-second gap turned an ordinary
# 100 GB fixture fall into "FALLING at 496862.1 GB/hour". In production that gap is
# a launchd job firing twice in quick succession, or a person running --check a
# moment after the timer did.
#
# 60 s against a declared 15-minute interval: a fifteenth of the cadence, so a
# tick that arrives early is never refused, while anything that looks like a
# double-fire is. And unlike the maximum gap, the minimum fails in the DANGEROUS
# direction if it is missing — a false alarm rather than a silent one — which is
# why it is declared rather than left to a sanity check.
DISK_DROP_MIN_GAP_SECONDS="60"

# THE CEO is notified on the machine itself, later than Rich, because a
# notification interrupts a person.
#
# DISK_CEO_NOTIFY_GB / DISK_CEO_URGENT_GB — a macOS user notification at each.
# His words: "at 50 GB free and again at 25 GB". 50 is "look at this today";
# 25 is "this will stop working".
DISK_CEO_NOTIFY_GB="50"
DISK_CEO_URGENT_GB="25"

# DISK_CEO_REPEAT_HOURS — how long before the same level notifies him again
# while the condition still holds. His words: "repeated once a day while the
# condition holds". 24 hours. A notification that repeats every 15 minutes is
# one he turns off, and then the 25 GB one never arrives.
DISK_CEO_REPEAT_HOURS="24"

# DISK_SCALE_EXTRA_VOLUMES — whether an extra volume's thresholds are scaled by
# its capacity relative to the primary's (1) or taken as the same absolute GB
# (0). 1, per addendum 3's "same thresholds scaled to its size".
#
# THE ARITHMETIC, written out so nobody has to trust it: the primary measures
# 460 GiB, so 60/50/25 GB are 13.0%, 10.9% and 5.4% of it. /Volumes/E1TB is # machine-path-exempt: retained historical measurement or design record; never a storage default
# 954 GiB, so the same fractions are about 124 GB, 104 GB and 52 GB. 25 GB free
# on a 954 GB disk is a far more urgent situation than 25 GB on a 460 GB one,
# which is why the absolute numbers are not simply reused.
DISK_SCALE_EXTRA_VOLUMES="1"

# DISK_WATCHDOG_MINUTES — the launchd interval. 15 minutes, his suggestion, and
# it is also what makes DISK_DROP_ALERT_GB a rate: the drop threshold is only
# meaningful relative to the gap between readings.
DISK_WATCHDOG_MINUTES="15"

# Alert attribution measures effective scratch, cache and state directories
# plus existing OS/developer candidates. An absent path is skipped.
#
# A DECLARED CANDIDATE LIST RATHER THAN A WALK OF THE VOLUME, and only measured
# WHEN AN ALERT IS FIRING. `du` over a 460 GB volume takes minutes; this job runs
# every 15 minutes and must cost nothing on a healthy machine, so the cheap check
# (one `df`) always runs and the expensive attribution runs only when there is
# something to attribute. A watchdog that costs real I/O every quarter hour is
# one somebody uninstalls.
#
# /private/tmp ADDED 2026-09-18, and its absence was Frank's D13. He forced an
# alert and it named `71.9 GB /Users/alex/ab`, `22.2 GB /Volumes/E1TB/vm` and # machine-path-exempt: retained historical measurement or design record; never a storage default
# `10.3 GB /Users/alex/.claude` — while 25.77 GiB sat in /private/tmp, larger
# than the second and third entries on that list, unable to appear at all because
# the list did not contain it. An attribution list that cannot name the biggest
# pile sends whoever reads it to the wrong directory on the one day it matters.
#
# OTHER APPS' CONTAINER FOLDER REMOVED 2026-09-25, and it must never come back.
# It was the ninth entry here, and it was what put up "python3.14 would like to
# access data from other apps" on the CEO's screen: the unified log shows
# `du(62614) deny(1) file-read-data .../Containers/com.apple.VoiceMemos`, then
# Mail, Messages, Notes, Safari, Maps, Home, Stocks and News, each attributed
# to this job's python3.14. Clicking Allow did not stick ("Session scoped auth
# is invalid for client"), so it prompted again on every alerting run. macOS
# protects other apps' data; measuring it is not ours to do. disk-watchdog.py
# refuses any entry that enters or holds that data (lib/foreign_app_data.py),
# so re-adding it here, or adding $HOME or ~/Library, is skipped and reported,
# and disk-watchdog.test.sh W22 fails. Record:
# richos-hq/docs/verification/2026-09-25-app-data-prompts/README.md.

# DISK_STATE_JSON — where the current reading and alert state are published for
# the RichOS app to read (addendum 1: "you expose the watchdog's current reading
# and alert state in a small JSON file the app can read (say where)"). The
# app-side timeline line that consumes it is the app engineer's.
DISK_STATE_JSON="$HOME/.claude/state/disk-watchdog.json"
